WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.030
- My name's Daniel Botsman.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:10.080
I teach in the history
department here at Yale,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.500
and it's a great pleasure to
be able to welcome you all

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.460
today to Yale's 23rd
annual John W. Hall lecture

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:18.723
in Japanese studies.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.450
As explained in these pamphlets

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.200
that hopefully you've all received.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:29.750
The series, the whole series
was established in 1999

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:34.620
with the generous
support of Mrs Robin Hall

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.200
to honor the memory of
her husband, John W. Hall,

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.690
who was of course one of
the most important figures

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.820
in the development of Japanese studies

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.103
in the decades following World War II.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.850
Professor Hall was a historian

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.040
primarily of pre-modern Japan,

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.320
who completed his PhD dissertation

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.370
at Harvard writing about Tanuma Okitsugu,

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.610
one of the important political figures

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.233
of the 18th century in Japan.

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:03.570
And he wrote the dissertation

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.030
under the direction of Edwin Reischauer.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.670
He then began his scholarly
career at the University

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:13.470
of Michigan in 1949 before
moving to Yale in 1961

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.443
as the A. Whitney Griswald
professor of history.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.380
As a scholar, he played a
critical role in opening the study

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.710
of pre-modern Japanese history
to the English speaking world

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.660
with a slew of important works,

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:32.070
including most importantly
perhaps his 1966 monograph,

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:36.540
government and local power
in Japan, 500 to 1700.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.870
He also, of course, edited
the original Cambridge history

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:43.680
of Japan volumes and enduring
contribution to the field,

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:46.923
which is only now in the
process of being updated.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.220
For all of his deep
engagement with the past,

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:53.430
he was also very much
an institution builder,

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.010
strongly committed to
the work of facilitating

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.663
the scholarship of the future.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:00.900
At Yale, he was chair of
the history department

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.150
and of the Council on
East Station studies.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:06.960
He was also master as it was
then known of Morse College,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.890
and he trained a generation
of graduate students here

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:12.870
who went on to reshape the field,

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.880
including figures such as Harper Litho,

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.883
Jeffrey Mass, James
McClain, and Suzanne Gay.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.280
Outside of Yale,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.790
he played a central role in
building the institutional

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.040
structures that have
supported the study of Japan

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.710
and Asia more generally ever since,

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:32.403
including the Association for
Asian Studies, for example.

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:37.980
Each year then with the exception
I will say of 2020, 21',

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:40.623
we took a little COVID break.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.330
Yale's current faculty have
the privilege of extending

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:46.920
to an eminent scholar of Japan,

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.370
an invitation to deliver
the whole lecture.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.280
And we're extremely fortunate
this year to have been able

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.600
to convince Professor Richard
J. Samuels Ford International

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.190
Professor of Political Science

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:02.010
at the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.980
to travel to the wilds
of New Haven to speak

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.033
to us all today.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:11.070
I can honestly think of no
active scholar in any discipline,

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.170
better qualified to deliver

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:16.050
the whole lecture in Japanese studies.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:17.880
He is a towering figure,

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:20.250
and I don't mean just because
he's actually like me,

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:23.520
quite tall, and a former
basketball player,

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.023
although that is also true.

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:29.340
Professor Samuels was born
in Brooklyn and grew up

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:33.090
just across the sound in
Long Island, on Long Island.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:35.250
While in high school
he met his wife Debra,

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.190
who we're also extremely pleased

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:40.710
to be able to welcome to New Haven.

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:42.159
Hi, welcome Debra.

00:03:42.159 --> 00:03:43.890
(audience applause)

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.990
If you're not already familiar with them,

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:49.290
I would recommend Debra Samuel's,

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:50.970
Japanese and Korean cookbooks,

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:54.480
which I think are among
the very best available

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.783
to introduce those cuisines
to people in English.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.110
And I hope you won't mind me saying that.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:02.040
As college students,

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:07.040
they went to Japan together
for the first time to Kyushu,

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:08.790
which is a place that
I know some people here

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:10.503
have a special connection to also.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:15.300
And then after that initial
experience, professor Samuels

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.700
went on to complete an MA at Tufts

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.950
and then his PhD at MIT where he has,

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:23.970
of course gone on to build
an illustrious career.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:27.810
Like John W. Hall, Professor
Samuels is very much

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.180
an institution builder who has held

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.010
many significant leadership roles,

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:34.110
not only at MIT, where he has served

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:39.110
in many important roles,
including as the director

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:43.110
of the Center for International Studies,

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:46.170
but also in numerous
organizations responsible

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:49.920
for stewarding different
aspects of US Japan relations

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.700
in particular, including the
Japan US Friendship Commission,

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.460
the Japan Committee of the
National Research Council,

00:04:56.460 --> 00:04:58.050
and the US Japan Conference

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.543
on Cultural and Educational Interchange.

00:05:01.525 --> 00:05:04.140
The contributions are, of course,

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.630
these contributions are of
course more than matched

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.750
by his achievements as a scholar.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:13.500
In the age of Wikipedia
and indeed ChatGPT,

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.600
there is I think no need for me to list

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.570
all of Professor Samuel's
important publications.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:22.353
I suspect ChatGPT might
add a few creative ones.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:25.530
But given the occasion,

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:29.790
it would be remiss of me not
to mention that his 1994 book,

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.520
Rich Nation Strong Army National Security

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:35.130
and the Technological
Transformation of Japan

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:38.910
was recipient of the John W. Hall Prize

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:41.190
from the Association of Asian Studies

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:43.890
for best book in the
field of Japanese studies

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:46.233
among other distinguished prizes.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.040
More generally, let me say that in an era

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:52.470
in which all disciplines seem to battle

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:55.020
with the curse of over specialization,

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.680
professor Samuel's corpus
of scholarly work speaks

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.070
not just across subfields
but across disciplines.

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.590
I certainly think it's true
that there's no Japan political

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:07.140
scientist whose work is
assigned in history classes

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:10.230
as often as those of Professor Samuels.

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.780
And part of the reason
for that is simply that

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:17.780
he always takes on topics of
clear and obvious importance.

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:20.550
Think for example, of his books on 311

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.413
or on Japanese brand strategy.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:25.110
For all of the seriousness

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.300
with which Professor Samuels engages

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.710
the specificities of Japan's
political culture moreover,

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.920
he's equally someone who thinks
broadly and comparatively

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.640
as perhaps most obviously evidenced

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:40.980
by his pathbreaking comparative
study of political leaders

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:44.043
in Japan and Italy,
Machiavelli's children.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:49.950
Professor Samuels is also a
wordsmith of unusual skill

00:06:49.950 --> 00:06:53.970
and talent whose books
are replete with sentences

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.880
that make you smile as well as think,

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:59.490
and which are full of
smaller treasures of wisdom,

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:03.240
as well as compelling
answers to large questions.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:06.690
Finally, let me say that
Professor Samuel's work

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:10.560
is characterized not only by
a refusal to pull punches,

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:13.800
but also by a level of
honesty and integrity,

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.650
which commands respect even
from those who might disagree

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:20.670
or take very different stances
on key political questions.

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:22.890
His most recent book, Special Duty,

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:25.650
a History of the Japanese
Intelligence community

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:27.900
provides an excellent example of this.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.960
I'm sure I'm not the only
person who's been forced

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.750
to rethink their own
preconceptions about issues such as

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:38.610
the controversial 2013
designated states secret.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:40.740
Sorry, I knew I was gonna stumble on this.

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:43.350
Designated state's secret law

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:45.933
as a result of reading
that wonderful book.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:48.200
I've gone on much too long, I know,

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:51.810
but I hope some of what I've
said will help you understand

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:56.070
why I am really excited to
hear Professor Samuel's lecture

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:59.100
today, and I hope without
that you will now join with me

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:00.993
in welcoming him to the podium.

00:08:01.918 --> 00:08:04.918
(audience applause)

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:10.350
- Thanks very much, Danny.

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:12.930
It's a pleasure to be here and it's,

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:15.843
it's an honor really to
address you this afternoon,

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:17.910
not least because

00:08:17.910 --> 00:08:20.970
the lecture series pays
tribute to one of the nation's

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:24.780
and Yale's most distinguished
Japan specialist,

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:25.953
John Whitney Hall.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.580
Now, I'll be speaking
about Japan's response

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.460
to its security
environment this afternoon,

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:34.860
and it's a topic I suspect
that Professor Hall would've

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:38.700
found of interest in a
different security environment,

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.710
but would certainly a found of interest.

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:41.850
But before I go there,

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:45.607
I want to reflect a bit on
what brought me to this topic

00:08:45.607 --> 00:08:50.607
and to connect the journey to
my mentor Lucian, Lucian Pie.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.150
Steve, you were,

00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:56.760
Lucian was still around when
you were right. Okay, good.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.053
You'll enjoy this I hope.

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:01.800
He earned his PhD here at Yale,

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:03.990
so it's not entirely inappropriate.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:08.990
But for Lucian's Festschrift
my co-editor was Myron Wiener,

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:15.480
and I asked each of our authors to,

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:19.560
they were all comparativist
of one stripe or another to,

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:21.810
we asked them to reflect
on the political culture

00:09:21.810 --> 00:09:23.373
of foreign area studies.

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:26.673
Why do comparativist some comparativist

00:09:26.673 --> 00:09:28.500
pick certain topics and not others,

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:32.010
and what's the political
culture that what are they,

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:34.263
what are they, what are they bathing in?

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:39.025
Our presumption was that
Lucian would be very,

00:09:39.025 --> 00:09:41.820
would've been very comfortable with that,

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:46.110
namely that there are academic
norms and academic values

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:48.210
that stimulate and perhaps legitimate

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:52.290
some inquiry and preclude others.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:55.290
And that was, Lucian was
very comfortable with that.

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:57.630
It turned out a very easy case to make.

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:00.390
It was quite clear that the circumstances

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:01.893
of foreign politics,

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.450
that they shape our research agendas,

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:12.930
that was, that was, that was
not a hard thing to discover.

00:10:12.930 --> 00:10:17.610
One obvious example from the
Festschrift session was made

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:20.250
by one of his former, Lucian's
former students who was

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:24.990
a specialist on Soviet
politics and who explained to

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:29.250
an understanding audience
that some of the best,

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:31.750
that the Soviet politics
contributed very little

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.643
to the literature and
political participation. Duh.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.002
But another was that some
of the best conceptual work

00:10:40.002 --> 00:10:43.080
on social movements and ethnicity came

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.360
from the study of South Asia.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:48.900
Again, this all made perfect sense.

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:51.003
But as I dug into the Japanese case,

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:55.920
it was less obvious why
with the notable exception

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.893
of Chalmers Johnson and maybe Ronald Dor,

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:05.180
Japanese contributed
so little to political,

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:07.050
economic or social theory.

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:11.340
Why were we, I wondered theory
takers and not theory makers.

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:16.050
Why didn't we do what
specialists and other fields

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:18.720
of comparative inquiry did?

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:22.060
Was it something about
us as Japan scholars

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:26.410
or was it something about Japan?

00:11:27.814 --> 00:11:30.659
And it was a few short
steps from those questions

00:11:30.659 --> 00:11:33.630
that led to a larger one.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:35.610
Had we somehow been captured?

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:38.250
Were we had captured intelligentsia?

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:42.450
And if so, who was our captor?

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:47.450
Were we slaves to convention
or perhaps to politics?

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:51.213
And if it's the latter, did
we serve our own sovereign?

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:55.020
It was politics that was driving us.

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:57.390
And this is something that John Dower,

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:59.217
another great Japan specialist

00:11:59.217 --> 00:12:01.113
and a colleague of mine at MIT.

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:05.730
He once charged that, or
is it because they were,

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:08.400
we were captives to a foreign sovereign

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:13.400
as the so-called Japan bashers
of the 80s and 90s suggested?

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:18.780
Did the considerable resources
of that were made available

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:21.570
to Japan specialists by Japanese sources

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:25.593
dampen our enthusiasm to say
critical things about Japan?

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:29.850
Did the resources that were
made available to us by

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:33.120
the US government dampen our enthusiasm

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:35.013
to criticize the United States?

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:39.060
In short, I found it with no
that with no small regret,

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:42.423
that for decades on both
sides of the Pacific,

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:44.883
of the Pacific War,

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:50.160
the political culture of
Japanese political studies

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:52.650
in the United States had
corresponded much more directly

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:55.398
to the ebbs and flows
of bilateral relations

00:12:55.398 --> 00:12:59.313
with Japan and the, between
Japan and the United States,

00:12:59.313 --> 00:13:01.950
than to the unfettered
intellectual curiosity

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:03.570
of those of us who were studying

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:06.480
and our predecessors who
were studying the topic.

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:10.380
That is Japanese political
studies had lurched from one

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:12.450
to another intellectual fashion,

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:16.320
and we likely missed a lot in the process.

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:17.340
I know I did.

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:20.790
I missed a lot and I'll get
to that point immediately.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:22.650
But so let me indulge in sort of an,

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:26.610
a personal account because
it wasn't until 30 years

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:29.610
after Lucian's Festschrift was published.

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:30.720
It was two decades

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:34.710
after I wrote Rich Nation Strong Army

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:35.940
that Danny referred to.

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:39.630
And at least seven to eight
years after securing Japan,

00:13:39.630 --> 00:13:42.180
the book on Japan's
grand strategy came out

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:46.023
that I realized that I had written two,

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:49.890
two books about Japanese
national security without

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:53.040
interrogating two of the
most important topics

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:54.780
of that domain.

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:59.250
Completely ignored how
Japan does intelligence.

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:03.420
And I completely ignored
in those two studies,

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:06.030
the prospects for Japan's nuclear breakout

00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:08.700
and acquisition of nuclear weapons.

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:11.160
And indeed in the business
of the Japanese state,

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:14.280
an earlier book that was not

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:16.800
on national security per se.

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:20.220
It had a very fact chapter on
nuclear power in the history

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:24.870
of the nuclear power industry
in Japan without ever once

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:28.230
my exploring what Japan was planning

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:29.730
to do with all that plutonium,

00:14:30.774 --> 00:14:35.774
that it was producing or
why it needed have the,

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:41.450
basically the a full
cycle nuclear capability.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.790
So about a decade ago when
it hit me that I had missed

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:49.770
all that stuff, I began
to wonder why I had failed

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:52.317
to address those topics and why
no one pointed it out to me.

00:14:52.317 --> 00:14:55.050
And I noticed that virtually
no one else had tackled

00:14:55.050 --> 00:14:57.180
those issues either,

00:14:57.180 --> 00:14:58.440
which is consistent with the point

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:00.600
I was starting with here with you.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:02.160
So I've spent the last decade trying

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.680
to backfill both topics.

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:08.880
And that brings me back to Lucian Pie.

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:11.110
I wrote a piece recently for

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:15.090
a really interesting volume

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:19.290
that was edited by Connie Ryer
and Nora Cottman in Germany.

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:23.850
And as I told a group at Columbia earlier,

00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:28.850
well late last year, that
and Charlie knows this well,

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:30.963
he was there.

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:35.790
When passing graduate
students in the hallway,

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:37.800
Lucian used to stop the students

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:42.800
and ask invariably, are
you being brave scholar?

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:44.973
Are you being brave?

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:51.330
And we would all nonplus not
know exactly how to respond

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:52.163
to such a question.

00:15:52.163 --> 00:15:54.720
It seemed bizarre to us at the time.

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:56.883
And we all smiled and we walked on.

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:02.160
But I have never forgotten the question.

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:05.460
And I realize now that he wasn't joking.

00:16:05.460 --> 00:16:08.790
You need to be brave because brave

00:16:08.790 --> 00:16:10.590
and now certainly brave means many things,

00:16:10.590 --> 00:16:12.990
but it appears as a choice
in one form or another

00:16:12.990 --> 00:16:17.310
at nearly every stage of
a major project in our,

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:19.143
in what we do as academics.

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:23.850
Starting with our choice
of dissertation topics.

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:26.490
But it's easier to hue seems to me,

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:30.060
to disciplinary norms
and to take on subjects

00:16:30.060 --> 00:16:33.107
that have already been
validated by our mentors and to,

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:36.900
and our peers than it is to
get out ahead of the subfield

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:38.520
or even the discipline.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.860
And that was, that's been
especially the problem

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:43.140
in Japanese studies, it seems to me.

00:16:43.140 --> 00:16:44.010
But in the current issue

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:45.900
of the Texas National Security Review,

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:49.980
a former colleague of
ours at MIT, Frank Gavin,

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:52.200
speaks directly to this issue

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:54.787
and I want to just quote him.

00:16:54.787 --> 00:16:57.007
"The public minded scholar is obliged

00:16:57.007 --> 00:16:58.833
"to disturb the status quo.

00:17:00.247 --> 00:17:02.827
"She should complicate stylized narrative,

00:17:02.827 --> 00:17:04.417
"undermine the conventional wisdom,

00:17:04.417 --> 00:17:06.937
"make people uncomfortable,
and most of all,

00:17:06.937 --> 00:17:11.277
"force them to challenge the
assumptions and to think."

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:14.460
And we all know how to review
an existing literature.

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:16.260
We know how to identify a narrow niche

00:17:16.260 --> 00:17:19.050
in which to drill down
for new information.

00:17:19.050 --> 00:17:21.390
We spend a lot of time in
graduate school learning

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:24.990
how to search for and apply
other people's models.

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:27.300
We answer other people's questions,

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:29.820
but it's much more difficult
and much more risky

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:32.760
to formulate new questions
and explore the previously

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:33.870
unexplored in new ways.

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:38.400
And we often fail, and I
have often failed to do that.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:40.980
Solutions, brave scholars
attempt to hack through

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:44.100
the undergrowth of difficult
problems with new tools

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:47.970
and especially with new
questions, important questions.

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:50.190
This is difficult and it's also risky.

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:53.220
And as Gavin points out,
also very unpopular.

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:56.070
You don't get to go to the
garden parties quite as often.

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:00.360
And the risks are greater
when the problem's tougher

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:02.460
or when the conventional wisdom is thicker

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:08.370
because taking risks
involves breaking China.

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:10.533
It's a small city, breaking China.

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:14.040
I'm not here to warmonger.

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:15.075
But this is,

00:18:15.075 --> 00:18:16.980
this is because there were
rules and norms in every field.

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:19.680
Again, this is back to that Festschrift.

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:21.540
And not least of all in Japanese studies

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:26.540
where rule number one is
decorum, polite, be polite.

00:18:26.790 --> 00:18:28.980
Observing decorum may not be difficult,

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:32.073
but it does constrain
creativity it seems to me.

00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:33.863
In my own case,

00:18:33.863 --> 00:18:36.840
this challenge was most clearly manifest

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:39.270
when I asked my Todi Unche,

00:18:39.270 --> 00:18:41.970
my professor at Tokyo University

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:44.400
for an affiliation with
his new research center.

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:46.020
I was doing rich nations,

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:48.570
what turned into rich nation, strong army.

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:52.740
And I wanted to go where the
cutting edge of work was being

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:57.060
done in Japan, and it was in
his center and he declined.

00:18:57.060 --> 00:19:01.410
He said, no, you can't affiliate
with us because he said,

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:03.396
my topic was,

00:19:03.396 --> 00:19:04.590
and something he had organized

00:19:04.590 --> 00:19:06.960
an important study group
on which I knew of.

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:11.960
He said it was too sensitive
and, (speaking Japanese)

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:14.950
the guys upstairs meaning the bureaucracy

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:18.420
wouldn't be comfortable with it.

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:21.180
And then after situating myself elsewhere,

00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:24.000
I asked for a copy of
the group's report and I,

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:25.053
he didn't provide it.

00:19:26.190 --> 00:19:28.680
Since I had followed all the decarus rules

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:33.030
of Japanese studies. (speaking Japanese)

00:19:33.030 --> 00:19:36.930
The gift exchanges and the
more substantive accommodations

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:37.763
over many years.

00:19:37.763 --> 00:19:40.170
And since he was supposed
to be an academic,

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:42.480
I thought we were in the same business.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:43.680
I was shocked.

00:19:43.680 --> 00:19:45.480
In the event I wrote the
book I wanted to write,

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:49.380
and it won several prizes,
as Danny kindly said,

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:53.220
including the John Whitney
Hall prize, which was lovely.

00:19:53.220 --> 00:19:56.283
But I found myself excluded
from a range of activities,

00:19:57.560 --> 00:20:00.810
a range of activities
for at least a decade.

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:03.600
And so Lucian pies in
junction to be brave,

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:07.053
which I never saw myself
as understanding before,

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:09.390
stayed in my head.

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:10.230
And it's still there.

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:13.410
There are still a lot of
questions we have not thought

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:15.480
to ask for a variety of reasons,

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:16.980
some of which I've tried to outline here

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:18.600
in my introductory remarks.

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:20.370
But it's, this is even more important.

00:20:20.370 --> 00:20:25.370
It seems to me when, as in
political science methods

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:26.783
are getting more,

00:20:26.783 --> 00:20:28.620
at least I can speak to
political science methods

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:33.000
are getting more attention
than substance in some cases.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:36.750
So let's turn to substance if we can.

00:20:36.750 --> 00:20:38.910
Actually, what was on the poster is,

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:41.400
is the substance that
I want to talk about.

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:45.720
I think that students of
national security who were born

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:49.980
after the Asia Pacific War
find it particularly difficult

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:54.450
to suspend disbelief about the
prospects for war and peace

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:57.873
in contemporary Asia,
particularly contemporary Japan.

00:20:59.340 --> 00:21:01.830
And, that's what I'd like
to discuss this afternoon,

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:05.490
not least because the
views of Japan's leaders

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:09.090
in Japanese public opinion
have clearly shifted

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:11.223
in the last decade or two.

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:15.210
The way scholars of international
relations get at this

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:17.490
is to work par paradigmatically.

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:21.270
This is old stuff to
many of you, but it's,

00:21:21.270 --> 00:21:24.900
it's just useful as a way in

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:29.285
which is to talk about the
paradigms very, very briefly.

00:21:29.285 --> 00:21:32.520
So I apologize in advance
for oversimplifying,

00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:34.740
but let me just suggest
that the constructivist,

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:37.560
the liberal and the realist
perspectives have each

00:21:37.560 --> 00:21:42.560
had their day in Japan's
post-war journey to the present.

00:21:44.070 --> 00:21:45.120
And by the present,

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:49.050
I'm referring to the most recent
announcement of Japan's new

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:53.910
national security strategy in
which was just in December,

00:21:53.910 --> 00:21:56.747
and I'll just refer to
it going forward now

00:21:56.747 --> 00:22:00.360
as the NSS, National Security Strategy.

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:02.160
So let me start by
flicking at each of these,

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:05.220
these three paradigms just
to frame the root Tokyo

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:09.150
has taken vis-a-vis the
alliance with the United States.

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:13.380
And I'll start with the
constructivist notion that to some

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:15.660
a post-war Japan has been pacifist

00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:19.383
as a matter of national
identity after the war.

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:22.800
You know, you're familiar with
how article nine stipulates

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:26.730
that Japan will not maintain
land, sea or air forces,

00:22:26.730 --> 00:22:29.370
and you are no doubt familiar
with how decades of so-called

00:22:29.370 --> 00:22:32.850
anti militarists public opinion was shaped

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:36.573
by the ghosts of militarists
past. And this is not untrue.

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:40.140
This actually, this
constructivist notion does explain

00:22:40.140 --> 00:22:41.910
a lot of that I think.

00:22:41.910 --> 00:22:44.710
I sometimes refer to the
shroud of, (speaking Japanese).

00:22:47.010 --> 00:22:49.680
You trying to get Japan away

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:52.380
from militarists, militarists past.

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:54.060
And pacifism, of course,

00:22:54.060 --> 00:22:56.490
was enshrined in law and declared

00:22:56.490 --> 00:23:00.933
to be Japan's national
identity at least after 1945.

00:23:01.860 --> 00:23:04.140
And anti militarists identity was invoked

00:23:04.140 --> 00:23:07.950
to constrain any Japanese
military capabilities,

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:11.130
particularly offensive ones like missiles

00:23:11.130 --> 00:23:15.870
and carriers and airborne
refueling and things like that.

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:18.840
The identity also included
so-called nuclear allergy,

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:21.540
which made it impossible to even imagine.

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:23.040
This is where I fell down,

00:23:23.040 --> 00:23:25.860
where it made it impossible
to even imagine Japan

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:27.240
having thought about

00:23:27.240 --> 00:23:30.690
in the acquisition of nuclear
power, nuclear weapons.

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:32.913
But it did. We now know that.

00:23:33.930 --> 00:23:36.420
And they talk about it openly now.

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:38.970
But this identity also included, as I say,

00:23:38.970 --> 00:23:42.660
the nuclear allergy,
a ban on arms exports,

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:45.000
an undersized intelligence capability,

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:49.350
and a prohibition on the
military use of space.

00:23:49.350 --> 00:23:51.030
It seemed that as a result,

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:54.963
for five decades or so
after the Asia-Pacific War,

00:23:56.460 --> 00:23:58.620
any serious discussion
of national security

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:01.920
was a dangerous third world, third rail

00:24:01.920 --> 00:24:06.920
for an ambitious Japanese
political candidate.

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:10.470
Now, what I wanna do is
just shift over slightly

00:24:10.470 --> 00:24:15.470
and connect it, this idea
into the liberal paradigm

00:24:16.470 --> 00:24:19.023
so we can interrogate this
identity more closely.

00:24:21.750 --> 00:24:23.460
As you know, Japanese conservatives

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:26.400
have governed Japan
virtually without a break

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:28.290
since the end of the US occupation.

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:31.015
It's well understood that
this conservative camp,

00:24:31.015 --> 00:24:33.180
I should say it's less
well understood that

00:24:33.180 --> 00:24:36.450
this conservative camp
was never monolithic.

00:24:36.450 --> 00:24:39.540
And for most of the second
half of the 20th century,

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:41.490
Japan followed the preferences

00:24:41.490 --> 00:24:46.360
of its greatest post-war
statesman, which was Joshi Daigaku.

00:24:47.543 --> 00:24:50.310
The LDP was dominated by
his wing of the party,

00:24:50.310 --> 00:24:54.600
which was a very pragmatic
wing of the LDP, the liberal.

00:24:54.600 --> 00:24:55.800
He was not in the LDP.

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:58.620
He left politics before the LDP was formed

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:01.830
in November of 55', 1955.

00:25:01.830 --> 00:25:06.693
But his school really
governed, for the most part,

00:25:08.310 --> 00:25:10.413
for the next 20, 30 years.

00:25:11.640 --> 00:25:14.880
The Yoshida doctrine, his
idea about national security

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:17.010
was brilliant politically.

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:19.530
It sustained the pragmatist
power and for the most part

00:25:19.530 --> 00:25:22.950
kept military realists,
who I'll get to in a moment

00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:23.943
at arm's length.

00:25:25.110 --> 00:25:29.520
Japan constrained itself,
self-imposed constraints

00:25:29.520 --> 00:25:31.760
and sheltered under US protection

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:34.230
and invoked its pacifist identity.

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:37.470
In doing so, it maneuvered
cleverly to avoid entanglement

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:39.963
in US wars, and there were many of those.

00:25:41.371 --> 00:25:45.360
Now, it enabled Japan
to get back on its feet

00:25:45.360 --> 00:25:47.160
without overextending itself

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:50.403
through defense, defense
spending. A conscious choice.

00:25:51.390 --> 00:25:52.410
And it was a choice in,

00:25:52.410 --> 00:25:55.290
from a liberal perspective of
interest groups fighting with

00:25:55.290 --> 00:25:58.170
one another about what
will drive the engine

00:25:58.170 --> 00:26:00.510
of post-war reconstruction.

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:01.470
Would it be defense?

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:03.840
Has the defense industry
wanted with Metis,

00:26:03.840 --> 00:26:06.270
or would it be everything else,

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:08.190
which is what the finance ministry wanted?

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:09.340
It was everything else.

00:26:11.259 --> 00:26:14.730
It made economic rehabilitation
Japan's priority.

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:18.030
Japan would remain lightly
armed and would avoid becoming

00:26:18.030 --> 00:26:21.019
too involved with
international strategic affairs

00:26:21.019 --> 00:26:23.790
while taking advantage
of US security guarantees

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:25.890
and getting rich from access

00:26:25.890 --> 00:26:29.610
to robust US markets and technology.

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:32.100
Now, politically, the
greatest benefit was that

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:36.690
the Japanese public and
Japan's neighbors both

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:40.080
would be reassured that
Japan no longer posed

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:44.640
a threat to them, to the home, to the home

00:26:44.640 --> 00:26:46.983
or to the neighbors.

00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:51.960
In exchange, Japan would
provide the United States

00:26:51.960 --> 00:26:54.660
forward basis, it still does.

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:58.500
So Japan's cheap writing
was in everyone's interests

00:26:58.500 --> 00:27:00.630
and became a blueprint
for Japanese defense

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:04.050
and foreign policy efforts
for the next half century.

00:27:04.050 --> 00:27:05.943
Seen in this, in this liberal view,

00:27:06.869 --> 00:27:10.830
it was not built on a national identity,

00:27:10.830 --> 00:27:14.109
rather on a political calculation.

00:27:14.109 --> 00:27:17.551
This was about domestic politics.

00:27:17.551 --> 00:27:19.827
And by the early 2000s

00:27:19.827 --> 00:27:23.670
LDP pragmatists ran outta steam, it seems,

00:27:23.670 --> 00:27:26.190
and the party's
nationalists gained momentum

00:27:26.190 --> 00:27:28.240
in their long and very erratic march

00:27:29.109 --> 00:27:33.540
to consolidating power and
controlling Japan's foreign

00:27:33.540 --> 00:27:34.650
and security policies.

00:27:34.650 --> 00:27:36.633
And moreover, by this time,

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:42.060
domestic squabbles within
the LDP had been overtaken

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:45.090
by marked shifts in Japan's
security environment,

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:47.760
a new world order was forming.

00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:51.540
So given that it's a new world
order and balances of power

00:27:51.540 --> 00:27:55.323
are changing, I mean, great
segue to the realist paradigm.

00:27:57.240 --> 00:27:59.460
Even before the end of the Cold War,

00:27:59.460 --> 00:28:04.391
Japan had begun to salami slice
away piece by piece slowly

00:28:04.391 --> 00:28:08.850
the self-imposed constraints
of the Yoshida doctrine.

00:28:08.850 --> 00:28:12.810
In the 1980s, they extended
the sea lines of communication,

00:28:12.810 --> 00:28:15.390
out, defense of the sea
lanes of communication out

00:28:15.390 --> 00:28:16.530
a thousand nautical miles.

00:28:16.530 --> 00:28:18.390
That's not terribly distant,

00:28:18.390 --> 00:28:20.760
but symbolically very important.

00:28:20.760 --> 00:28:21.690
In the 1990s,

00:28:21.690 --> 00:28:24.287
they sent mine sweepers
to the Persian Gulf after

00:28:24.287 --> 00:28:25.743
the conflict ended.

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:31.730
But de facto they had
military use of space.

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:32.850
By the 2000S,

00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:37.140
they were sending destroyers
to escort tankers,

00:28:37.140 --> 00:28:41.130
to Diego Garcia to fuel
us and British war planes

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:45.750
to fight operation enduring
freedom in Afghanistan.

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:48.780
And the salami slicing simply accelerated.

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:53.780
It really ramped up in the
2010s and into this decade.

00:28:53.970 --> 00:28:57.060
Collective self-defense became
possible reinterpretation

00:28:57.060 --> 00:29:01.230
of the constitution to allow
Japan's military to protect

00:29:01.230 --> 00:29:05.760
the American military
should have come under fire,

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:07.590
and under other kinds of conditions,

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:08.550
which we can talk about.

00:29:08.550 --> 00:29:12.586
But they built a naval facility in Jabuti

00:29:12.586 --> 00:29:15.150
for 10s of millions of dollars.

00:29:15.150 --> 00:29:19.110
They loosened their arms export,
control ban self-imposed.

00:29:19.110 --> 00:29:22.740
They created this new state
secrets law that Danny referred

00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:25.410
to, that we all got exercised about

00:29:25.410 --> 00:29:27.810
and realized that was not
such a terrible thing.

00:29:29.117 --> 00:29:34.110
Again, I felt the same way
that he did as I got into it.

00:29:34.110 --> 00:29:37.140
Stood up a cyber command. They
shifted to Southern defense.

00:29:37.140 --> 00:29:39.453
They stood up a Marine
Corps, a Marine Corps.

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:43.620
That's an offensive capability
last time I checked.

00:29:43.620 --> 00:29:48.620
And an open discussion
of a nuclear deterrent.

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:52.890
And I haven't even come
to the current NSS yet,

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:54.180
but I'm getting there.

00:29:54.180 --> 00:29:56.640
I promise, I promise you I am.

00:29:56.640 --> 00:30:01.640
By 2021, the shift in East
Asia's balance of power

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:04.770
had become very clear to most Japanese.

00:30:04.770 --> 00:30:08.430
And with that, so had the
salience of national security

00:30:08.430 --> 00:30:10.473
in domestic Japanese politics.

00:30:11.610 --> 00:30:14.410
And that year, of 2021,

00:30:14.410 --> 00:30:17.910
three of four candidates for
the presidency of the LDP

00:30:17.910 --> 00:30:20.670
and therefore for the prime ministership

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:21.873
to succeed Sue Tsukasa.

00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:26.767
Three of the four of them
to be prime minister,

00:30:26.767 --> 00:30:28.500
emphasized defense issues
and said they were willing

00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:30.360
to raise the defense budget.

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:35.360
I mean, this was hetero
docs beyond repair,

00:30:35.665 --> 00:30:38.580
under earlier assumptions

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:41.550
about Japan's pacifist national identity.

00:30:41.550 --> 00:30:43.770
And three of the four
said they would do that.

00:30:43.770 --> 00:30:45.753
And the fourth one came in dead last.

00:30:46.620 --> 00:30:48.033
Okay, so this emboldened,

00:30:49.170 --> 00:30:52.080
it was a good reading
actually of the public,

00:30:52.080 --> 00:30:54.780
of changes in public opinion as well.

00:30:54.780 --> 00:30:57.900
Clearly, Japanese public
opinion on national security

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:00.870
had shifted in the direction of broader,

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:03.180
more realistic security discourse.

00:31:03.180 --> 00:31:07.200
And, we should credit Abe
Shinzo, prime minister,

00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:10.740
the late prime minister with part of that.

00:31:10.740 --> 00:31:14.440
But of course, his government
that is Abe's government

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:17.700
as he was trying to
push the Japanese public

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:20.280
into a more realistic posture

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:22.560
and appreciation for Japan's having

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:23.913
a more realistic pasture,

00:31:24.780 --> 00:31:28.740
had no little help from
two provocative neighbors,

00:31:28.740 --> 00:31:33.033
North Korea and China, and
as well as from Washington.

00:31:33.990 --> 00:31:36.660
And the help from Washington
is counterintuitive.

00:31:36.660 --> 00:31:39.360
The help from Washington with,

00:31:39.360 --> 00:31:42.570
came from what many Japanese came to,

00:31:42.570 --> 00:31:46.290
to openly fear was that the United States

00:31:46.290 --> 00:31:49.480
would simply not be as
committed to defending Japan

00:31:49.480 --> 00:31:52.260
as it had been before China's rise,

00:31:52.260 --> 00:31:55.350
and especially before North Korea acquired

00:31:55.350 --> 00:31:57.480
intercontinental ballistic missiles.

00:31:57.480 --> 00:31:58.890
It changed everything.

00:31:58.890 --> 00:32:02.793
And so the confidence in
the United States declined,

00:32:04.350 --> 00:32:06.270
especially with that first ICBM test

00:32:06.270 --> 00:32:08.613
by the North Koreans in 2017.

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:10.980
And now by last year,

00:32:10.980 --> 00:32:14.250
the majority of Japanese
polled on these issues

00:32:14.250 --> 00:32:16.740
viewed China as a threat and reporting,

00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:19.087
the reported feeling, and
this a quote from the survey.

00:32:19.087 --> 00:32:21.627
"That US influence will weaken."

00:32:22.950 --> 00:32:26.160
Now, that's realism 101 in a sense.

00:32:26.160 --> 00:32:29.760
Japan is no longer driving
its security bus with one foot

00:32:29.760 --> 00:32:34.110
on the brake. It's now become
a realist in realist clothing.

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:36.780
And this is a, this is a change.

00:32:36.780 --> 00:32:40.260
And what I want to talk
about in the time I have left

00:32:40.260 --> 00:32:44.430
are three realist oriented
questions that are core

00:32:44.430 --> 00:32:48.570
to the US Japan Alliance and
to Japanese national security.

00:32:48.570 --> 00:32:51.933
The first is how far Japan
would go to balance China.

00:32:52.890 --> 00:32:57.243
The second is how far Japan
would go to defend Taiwan.

00:32:58.110 --> 00:33:01.110
And the third is to what
extent is Japan hedging

00:33:01.110 --> 00:33:03.573
against abandonment by the United States?

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:06.030
Now to answer these questions,

00:33:06.030 --> 00:33:08.803
it helps to provide I think, more of a,

00:33:08.803 --> 00:33:11.910
a feel for the contours of
Japan's security environment

00:33:11.910 --> 00:33:13.560
as articulated in the NSS.

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:16.290
I told you I would get there, we're here.

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:19.113
The new NSS, which is the
first one in a decade,

00:33:21.720 --> 00:33:23.670
has attracted a great deal of attention.

00:33:23.670 --> 00:33:26.290
It's more blunt, it's more ambitious

00:33:27.420 --> 00:33:29.910
and it's more muscular than
anything that preceded it.

00:33:29.910 --> 00:33:34.140
And it's also occasionally
as opaque as all the,

00:33:34.140 --> 00:33:36.210
as the many things that preceded it.

00:33:36.210 --> 00:33:40.250
But by far the most
remarked upon innovation,

00:33:41.460 --> 00:33:43.680
let's call it, military innovation,

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:44.970
is its authorization of

00:33:44.970 --> 00:33:49.680
the new standoff missile
counterstrike capability.

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:51.690
In making the case for counterstrike,

00:33:51.690 --> 00:33:55.740
which the NSS goes to
great pains to insist,

00:33:55.740 --> 00:33:58.170
is not in violation of Article nine

00:33:58.170 --> 00:34:01.230
of the Japanese constitution,
nor Japan's principle

00:34:01.230 --> 00:34:03.273
of exclusively defensive defense.

00:34:04.770 --> 00:34:08.670
It declares existing BMD,
ballistic missile defense

00:34:08.670 --> 00:34:12.570
and missile capabilities simply
to be no longer sufficient

00:34:12.570 --> 00:34:16.230
to deter its adversaries.

00:34:16.230 --> 00:34:19.560
Its adversaries, means China, North Korea.

00:34:19.560 --> 00:34:24.560
And it, and to deter aggression
says the, says the report.

00:34:25.200 --> 00:34:28.710
Less clear though is which
missiles will be acquired.

00:34:28.710 --> 00:34:31.012
It looks like it's tomahawks

00:34:31.012 --> 00:34:34.860
and who will do the targeting
because Japan doesn't

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:39.860
have sophisticated missile
targeting capabilities yet.

00:34:40.200 --> 00:34:42.990
So this is especially problematic
because it's, as I say,

00:34:42.990 --> 00:34:46.050
it's C3I capabilities are
two underdeveloped for

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:49.440
the missiles to be credible as
an autonomous deterrent yet.

00:34:49.440 --> 00:34:54.000
So that looks like the United
States then plays a role.

00:34:54.000 --> 00:34:56.220
At least there are whispers
that the United States military

00:34:56.220 --> 00:34:58.710
will play, help play a role in targeting.

00:34:58.710 --> 00:35:01.020
And close behind the
counterstrike decision

00:35:01.020 --> 00:35:06.020
in media, inc spilled the NSS
now formally commits Japan

00:35:06.990 --> 00:35:10.800
to doubling its defense budget
as a percentage GDP over

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:12.630
the next five years.

00:35:12.630 --> 00:35:16.890
Now, it's not clear though
how an underfunded government

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:18.630
of Japan's gonna pay for all this.

00:35:18.630 --> 00:35:20.757
There's a lot, that's a lot of money.

00:35:20.757 --> 00:35:23.550
And so the other thing to notice is not,

00:35:23.550 --> 00:35:25.470
is that there's considerable opposition

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:28.230
inside the government, inside the LDP,

00:35:28.230 --> 00:35:32.163
the ruling, the ruling party to new taxes.

00:35:33.210 --> 00:35:36.630
This is, they don't wanna
raise taxes for this,

00:35:36.630 --> 00:35:38.010
they don't wanna wanna raise taxes at all

00:35:38.010 --> 00:35:40.410
'cause they wanna get
reelected and that's not,

00:35:40.410 --> 00:35:42.330
that's not irrational.

00:35:42.330 --> 00:35:44.460
But there's much speculation therefore,

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:47.670
that a lot of the spending,
the doubling of the GD,

00:35:47.670 --> 00:35:51.120
percentage of GDP will
not be new spending.

00:35:51.120 --> 00:35:56.120
So for example, there's this
wonderful prestidigitation

00:35:57.150 --> 00:35:59.580
by which the Japan Coast Guard

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:02.820
and some government funded
dual use R&amp;D budgets

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:05.730
are now gonna be swept
into the defense budget.

00:36:05.730 --> 00:36:09.750
That takes care of a
whole hunk of the increase

00:36:09.750 --> 00:36:11.190
in defense spending right there.

00:36:11.190 --> 00:36:14.253
Japan's Coast Guard is
a non-trivial force.

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:19.080
And it's not clear how Japan

00:36:19.080 --> 00:36:23.100
will spend whatever
funding is actually new,

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:26.670
like for the counter, the
counterstrike capability.

00:36:26.670 --> 00:36:31.670
Now third, the centrality of the alliance

00:36:31.888 --> 00:36:34.270
in the, in the NSF was reaffirmed

00:36:35.456 --> 00:36:38.910
and really earlier than
the NSF was reaffirmed

00:36:38.910 --> 00:36:43.209
by both countries, even
to the point of openly

00:36:43.209 --> 00:36:46.800
considering the use of combined forces

00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:50.793
in selected contingencies
in Northeast Asia.

00:36:52.470 --> 00:36:54.123
Biden administration loved that.

00:36:55.380 --> 00:36:58.193
They cheered when the NSS,
bluntly referred to China as the,

00:36:58.193 --> 00:37:02.827
this is a quote from the
national security strategy.

00:37:02.827 --> 00:37:05.137
"The greatest strategic
challenge to ensuring the peace

00:37:05.137 --> 00:37:08.190
"and security of Japan and
the international community."

00:37:08.190 --> 00:37:09.363
They pulled no punches.

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:14.883
And someone in Washington may
have gone hoarse cheering.

00:37:15.720 --> 00:37:18.960
It's the statement in the
NSS that Japan must respond

00:37:18.960 --> 00:37:22.710
to China's threats to the
rule-based international order

00:37:22.710 --> 00:37:24.630
in cooperation with Washington

00:37:24.630 --> 00:37:27.450
and "other like-minded countries."

00:37:27.450 --> 00:37:29.670
We'll get to those other
like-minded countries in a moment.

00:37:29.670 --> 00:37:32.220
But the, it also speaks
to the strategic use

00:37:32.220 --> 00:37:36.063
of overseas development assistance, aid,

00:37:37.170 --> 00:37:42.000
the strategic use of aid
openly in the document,

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:43.170
presumably in the form

00:37:43.170 --> 00:37:46.920
of increased transfers
of military equipment

00:37:46.920 --> 00:37:49.380
to naval vessels, for example,

00:37:49.380 --> 00:37:52.623
in aircraft to align
states in Southeast Asia.

00:37:54.030 --> 00:37:56.970
And finally, we can see
an interesting changes

00:37:56.970 --> 00:37:59.703
in how Japan's signature cons,

00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:03.060
interesting changes in
Japan's signature concept

00:38:03.060 --> 00:38:07.410
of comprehensive security
is characterized.

00:38:07.410 --> 00:38:08.970
Some of you in looking around the room,

00:38:08.970 --> 00:38:12.600
not many of you will
remember that in the 1980s,

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:15.360
Japanese strategists
associated with Yoshida

00:38:15.360 --> 00:38:17.550
and the Yoshida doctrine invented

00:38:17.550 --> 00:38:20.560
the term comprehensive security

00:38:21.930 --> 00:38:26.250
to justify low defense
spending because security

00:38:26.250 --> 00:38:29.190
included economics and
trade and technology.

00:38:29.190 --> 00:38:30.810
And therefore we shouldn't
spend so much money

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:35.019
on the military. Very interesting concept.

00:38:35.019 --> 00:38:39.390
And, they'd emphasize Japan's
commitments to diplomacy

00:38:39.390 --> 00:38:42.210
and foreign economic policy,
particularly energy policy.

00:38:42.210 --> 00:38:45.150
But today's NSS speaks instead

00:38:45.150 --> 00:38:47.910
to not comprehensive security,

00:38:47.910 --> 00:38:49.953
but to comprehensive national power.

00:38:51.240 --> 00:38:53.100
Not the same thing at all.

00:38:53.100 --> 00:38:56.130
And fore grounds the military alongside

00:38:56.130 --> 00:38:58.634
the intelligence community in the,

00:38:58.634 --> 00:39:03.634
in Japan's national security toolkit.

00:39:05.460 --> 00:39:09.030
Now, all this said, there was one large,

00:39:09.030 --> 00:39:13.440
very large dog that didn't
bark in any of this.

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:15.660
And that's of course what I've referred

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:17.703
to several times as a nuclear breakout.

00:39:20.730 --> 00:39:24.450
It didn't bark in the NSS, but it did bark

00:39:24.450 --> 00:39:26.073
after the Ukraine war started.

00:39:28.050 --> 00:39:31.080
Former prime minister Abe minced no words

00:39:31.080 --> 00:39:36.080
within three days, maybe four of the,

00:39:36.270 --> 00:39:41.270
the Russian aggression
into Ukraine invasion.

00:39:41.280 --> 00:39:43.530
He called on the government
to discuss the viability

00:39:43.530 --> 00:39:46.230
of nuclear sharing with the United States.

00:39:46.230 --> 00:39:48.280
He said we should have that conversation.

00:39:50.880 --> 00:39:52.230
But Prime Minister Kishida,

00:39:52.230 --> 00:39:55.773
who by the way some of you
will know is from Hiroshima.

00:39:56.735 --> 00:40:01.260
He passed on this and
it's his NSS after all.

00:40:01.260 --> 00:40:04.350
Now, his strategy, it's
his government strategy,

00:40:04.350 --> 00:40:05.790
his name is on it.

00:40:05.790 --> 00:40:08.460
And it states clearly that
there will be no change

00:40:08.460 --> 00:40:10.893
to Japan's three non-nuclear principles.

00:40:12.210 --> 00:40:15.270
Japan is and this is a direct quote from

00:40:15.270 --> 00:40:18.870
a really wonderful analysis by an Israeli,

00:40:18.870 --> 00:40:20.070
an Israeli analyst.

00:40:20.070 --> 00:40:22.413
He said, "Japan is the
most salient example

00:40:22.413 --> 00:40:27.413
"in the world of nuclear
hedging among global powers."

00:40:27.480 --> 00:40:30.030
And has already, as we know,

00:40:30.030 --> 00:40:34.950
openly embraced the deterrent
benefits as they see it,

00:40:34.950 --> 00:40:36.150
of nuclear latency.

00:40:36.150 --> 00:40:39.213
That is the, the nuclear hedge. Okay.

00:40:40.170 --> 00:40:43.770
In fact, a sitting
secretary of defense talked

00:40:43.770 --> 00:40:48.060
about after 311 the need to
maintain nuclear power in order

00:40:48.060 --> 00:40:51.905
to have, in order to have enough fuel

00:40:51.905 --> 00:40:55.300
and weapons grade materials
for nuclear weapons

00:40:56.190 --> 00:40:57.900
as a signal of latency.

00:40:57.900 --> 00:40:59.600
And that was a,

00:41:00.690 --> 00:41:03.033
that was as clear a
statement as you can get.

00:41:07.530 --> 00:41:09.750
But the NSS disingenuously,

00:41:09.750 --> 00:41:12.210
I thought declared its commitment

00:41:12.210 --> 00:41:14.943
to arms controlled disarmament
and non-proliferation.

00:41:16.140 --> 00:41:21.140
So it's in that nether world
of we could do this if we want

00:41:21.530 --> 00:41:23.880
to do it, we can talk
about this in the Q&amp;A.

00:41:23.880 --> 00:41:25.380
But we could do this if we wanna do it.

00:41:25.380 --> 00:41:27.060
We have the technology,
we have the equipment,

00:41:27.060 --> 00:41:30.660
we have the material,
but we don't wanna do it.

00:41:30.660 --> 00:41:33.630
Don't make us do it,
but don't play with us

00:41:33.630 --> 00:41:35.220
because we could do it and so forth.

00:41:35.220 --> 00:41:36.900
You get the idea.

00:41:36.900 --> 00:41:40.113
So nuclear arm and even
if it's German style,

00:41:41.850 --> 00:41:45.450
German style nuclear sharing
has been left for another day.

00:41:45.450 --> 00:41:46.620
I don't think it's entirely,

00:41:46.620 --> 00:41:49.410
I can't imagine that it's
entirely off the agenda

00:41:49.410 --> 00:41:54.030
if my reading of Japanese
concern about American commitment

00:41:54.030 --> 00:41:56.853
and capabilities is correct.

00:41:58.170 --> 00:42:01.140
So from a realist perspective,
nowhere is the shift

00:42:01.140 --> 00:42:04.080
of the regional balance
of power in China's favor,

00:42:04.080 --> 00:42:07.980
potentially more consequential
for Japan than Taiwan.

00:42:07.980 --> 00:42:10.530
To get to that second
issue I wanted to raise,

00:42:10.530 --> 00:42:13.290
which sits along the first
island chain at a strategic

00:42:13.290 --> 00:42:17.040
choke point for naval forces
in the Western Pacific

00:42:17.040 --> 00:42:20.973
and is critical to securing
Japan's southern flag.

00:42:22.440 --> 00:42:24.810
Tokyo, very long cautious

00:42:24.810 --> 00:42:27.870
in offering overt support to Taiwan,

00:42:27.870 --> 00:42:30.270
issued a defense white paper in 2021,

00:42:30.270 --> 00:42:32.554
declaring that Japan will,

00:42:32.554 --> 00:42:35.497
"Pay close attention to
the Taiwan's situation

00:42:35.497 --> 00:42:37.257
"with a sense of crisis."

00:42:38.520 --> 00:42:42.030
And that was followed by
a series of 2021, 2022

00:42:42.030 --> 00:42:46.350
trial balloons that were sent
up by senior Japanese defense

00:42:46.350 --> 00:42:50.100
officials who proclaimed that
Japan has to defend Taiwan,

00:42:50.100 --> 00:42:52.053
will have to defend Taiwan.

00:42:53.565 --> 00:42:56.475
I could give you the quotes, but for the,

00:42:56.475 --> 00:42:59.325
I won't go into them. They're
all basically that message.

00:43:00.990 --> 00:43:05.703
So it wasn't a huge surprise
that the NSS went bold on this.

00:43:07.140 --> 00:43:09.150
Although it declares this no change

00:43:09.150 --> 00:43:12.330
to the 1972 Japan China communique

00:43:12.330 --> 00:43:14.970
or to its relationship with Taiwan.

00:43:14.970 --> 00:43:18.597
The NSS stresses that Japan
and Taiwan "Share values,"

00:43:19.590 --> 00:43:20.947
and labels Taiwan,

00:43:20.947 --> 00:43:24.117
"An extremely important
partner and dear friend."

00:43:25.410 --> 00:43:27.180
Now these developments are striking,

00:43:27.180 --> 00:43:29.790
but given that Japan
has no treaty obligation

00:43:29.790 --> 00:43:31.053
to defend Taiwan,

00:43:32.040 --> 00:43:33.810
and given the economic stakes embedded

00:43:33.810 --> 00:43:36.930
in the China relationship,
which are considerable.

00:43:36.930 --> 00:43:39.573
China is Japan's largest
single trading partner.

00:43:41.550 --> 00:43:44.880
I'd guess that some Japanese
prime ministers like Yoshida

00:43:44.880 --> 00:43:48.540
would hesitate ordering
the SDF to fight in support

00:43:48.540 --> 00:43:52.200
of US forces in the
Taiwan contingency until,

00:43:52.200 --> 00:43:54.840
or unless there are there

00:43:54.840 --> 00:43:58.590
the Japanese civilians or
soldiers fall to hostile fire.

00:43:58.590 --> 00:44:00.840
So we'll watch the space
and remind ourselves

00:44:00.840 --> 00:44:02.430
that just a few years ago,

00:44:02.430 --> 00:44:07.230
even a general discussion
of these shifts and plans

00:44:07.230 --> 00:44:12.230
would've ensured a government
crumbling thunder clap.

00:44:12.720 --> 00:44:17.400
This time, crickets, crickets.

00:44:17.400 --> 00:44:22.110
Now this is possible for
two not unrelated reasons.

00:44:22.110 --> 00:44:25.080
First, because the US foreign
policy has been feeding

00:44:25.080 --> 00:44:27.238
an abandonment discourse.

00:44:27.238 --> 00:44:28.071
There's no other way to describe it.

00:44:28.071 --> 00:44:30.570
There's an abandonment
discourse in Japan now

00:44:30.570 --> 00:44:31.740
for several years.

00:44:31.740 --> 00:44:34.500
And second, because the center
of gravity of Japanese public

00:44:34.500 --> 00:44:36.150
opinion has shifted to the right.

00:44:37.650 --> 00:44:42.030
To test this, very crudely,

00:44:42.030 --> 00:44:43.743
I'm not known as a methodologist,

00:44:44.640 --> 00:44:46.320
but to test this very crudely,

00:44:46.320 --> 00:44:49.680
I took a quick look at how
the ragged US withdrawal

00:44:49.680 --> 00:44:53.037
from Afghanistan in August
of 2021 was connecting

00:44:55.980 --> 00:44:58.330
to the Japanese conversation about Taiwan

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:03.000
and about a Taiwan contingency.

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:06.030
And I found hundreds of thousands of links

00:45:06.030 --> 00:45:09.930
in a Japanese Google search
to stories, links to stories,

00:45:09.930 --> 00:45:14.433
asking questions like, will
Taiwan be like Afghanistan?

00:45:15.540 --> 00:45:20.130
Will Japan also be abandoned
by the United States?

00:45:20.130 --> 00:45:24.540
The American withdrawal
from Afghanistan galvanized

00:45:24.540 --> 00:45:26.130
attention in Japan.

00:45:26.130 --> 00:45:31.130
And predictably, very predictably,
the crisis in Ukraine,

00:45:31.200 --> 00:45:32.850
further stimulated these concerns.

00:45:32.850 --> 00:45:37.850
So what was a low boil of
concern about American commitment

00:45:38.130 --> 00:45:42.180
and capabilities and really
happy talk among alliances

00:45:42.180 --> 00:45:45.510
at formal meetings like
two plus twos and so forth.

00:45:45.510 --> 00:45:49.560
What was a low boil began to
bubble really quite vigorously.

00:45:49.560 --> 00:45:51.660
And so when I did the same Google search,

00:45:51.660 --> 00:45:55.323
but subs after the Ukraine crisis began.

00:45:56.790 --> 00:46:01.350
Google returned twice the
number of hits that it did on

00:46:01.350 --> 00:46:03.180
the Afghanistan question.

00:46:03.180 --> 00:46:05.640
And sifting through hundreds of them,

00:46:05.640 --> 00:46:08.220
it was clear to me that
many Japanese were fretting

00:46:08.220 --> 00:46:11.610
about whether failed US support
for Ukraine might undermine

00:46:11.610 --> 00:46:14.013
extended deterrence in Northeast Asia.

00:46:15.060 --> 00:46:16.920
So let's take a look at this concern

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:19.770
and take on that third question
that I mentioned, which is,

00:46:19.770 --> 00:46:21.775
what is Japan doing about it,

00:46:21.775 --> 00:46:25.620
about this by hedging, Japanese hedging?

00:46:25.620 --> 00:46:28.350
It's a classic realist question.

00:46:28.350 --> 00:46:31.620
But the blunt version,
blunt version is that

00:46:31.620 --> 00:46:34.170
while Japanese leaders
know that the US alliance

00:46:34.170 --> 00:46:37.623
is their best bet, there's
no question about that.

00:46:39.150 --> 00:46:44.040
Tokyo has been nonetheless
actively hedging for some time

00:46:44.040 --> 00:46:46.200
and is doing so more actively these days

00:46:46.200 --> 00:46:48.570
with very broad public support,

00:46:48.570 --> 00:46:50.970
and ironically with US encouragement.

00:46:50.970 --> 00:46:51.927
Very interesting,.

00:46:52.860 --> 00:46:55.170
The Russian invasion of
Ukraine made it very easy

00:46:55.170 --> 00:46:58.140
for Japan's hedging inclined realists.

00:46:58.140 --> 00:46:59.550
Just months ago,

00:46:59.550 --> 00:47:02.310
the chief of the ground
self-defense forces, well,

00:47:02.310 --> 00:47:04.350
the former chief of the
ground self-defense forces,

00:47:04.350 --> 00:47:06.570
the army, the Japanese army,

00:47:06.570 --> 00:47:08.670
made a very succinct
case for the connection

00:47:08.670 --> 00:47:12.120
between the Ukraine war
and further discussions

00:47:12.120 --> 00:47:14.280
of a nuclear weapons option.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:17.340
Was a former chief of
the Japanese military.

00:47:17.340 --> 00:47:21.037
He said, "The world learned from Ukraine

00:47:21.037 --> 00:47:24.217
"that the United States will
not intervene militarily

00:47:24.217 --> 00:47:26.397
"in the event of a possible nuclear war."

00:47:27.690 --> 00:47:32.250
That we were deterred from
getting in by Russia's possession

00:47:32.250 --> 00:47:36.450
and Putin's, you know, Putin's behavior,

00:47:36.450 --> 00:47:39.390
declarations and capabilities.

00:47:39.390 --> 00:47:43.320
So besides its agreements with Washington,

00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:48.060
Japan now has two plus twos,
that's defense and state,

00:47:48.060 --> 00:47:52.110
defense and foreign ministers
meeting together bilaterally

00:47:52.110 --> 00:47:53.640
with five countries.

00:47:53.640 --> 00:47:56.580
It has acquisition and
cross servicing agreements,

00:47:56.580 --> 00:47:59.010
the axes with four Gsomias,

00:47:59.010 --> 00:48:02.190
which are intelligence sharing agreements

00:48:02.190 --> 00:48:03.330
with seven countries,

00:48:03.330 --> 00:48:06.720
bilateral military training
with eight different countries

00:48:06.720 --> 00:48:09.300
and has ink defense
equipment transfer agreements

00:48:09.300 --> 00:48:11.640
with five different countries.

00:48:11.640 --> 00:48:12.540
And in recent years,

00:48:12.540 --> 00:48:15.480
there's been a noticeable
increase in the exercises

00:48:15.480 --> 00:48:19.830
and port visits to Japan by countries

00:48:19.830 --> 00:48:22.890
that are not the United States of America.

00:48:22.890 --> 00:48:24.060
From Japan's perspective,

00:48:24.060 --> 00:48:27.690
this is designed to keep
major states onside,

00:48:27.690 --> 00:48:30.600
and neighbors onside vis-a-vis China

00:48:30.600 --> 00:48:33.150
and to hedge against
what the MOD think tank.

00:48:33.150 --> 00:48:35.040
The MOD think tank, meaning NIDS,

00:48:35.040 --> 00:48:37.190
the National Institute
for Defense Studies.

00:48:38.040 --> 00:48:40.657
Openly has identified as the, like,

00:48:40.657 --> 00:48:43.657
"Likely shrinking of US relative power

00:48:43.657 --> 00:48:46.680
"in a multi-polar order."

00:48:46.680 --> 00:48:48.420
Now, some initiatives are more robust

00:48:48.420 --> 00:48:50.550
or more modest I should say, than others,

00:48:50.550 --> 00:48:52.860
but there have been developments
of note that have preceded

00:48:52.860 --> 00:48:57.030
in parallel with a tightened
hug of the United States.

00:48:57.030 --> 00:48:58.560
So I'm gonna just quickly flick at them

00:48:58.560 --> 00:49:00.360
and then I'll get out of your way.

00:49:00.360 --> 00:49:02.550
But the one that has
attracted the most attention

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:06.510
is the quad, known as
the quad, which comprises

00:49:06.510 --> 00:49:09.990
the United States,
Australia, Japan and India,

00:49:09.990 --> 00:49:14.110
four countries the chair
concerns about and seek

00:49:16.680 --> 00:49:21.540
to counter China's assertive
behavior in the Indo-Pacific.

00:49:21.540 --> 00:49:23.490
It's a vast area, of course,

00:49:23.490 --> 00:49:26.430
through which the world's
exports in more than a third

00:49:26.430 --> 00:49:30.120
of its global imports are transit.

00:49:30.120 --> 00:49:32.040
Now, this is not a NATO by any,

00:49:32.040 --> 00:49:36.060
it's not a mini NATO,
mini me by any any extent.

00:49:36.060 --> 00:49:40.500
But quad leaders do
hold regularly scheduled

00:49:40.500 --> 00:49:43.680
strategic consultations and
joint military exercises.

00:49:43.680 --> 00:49:48.180
It's the beginning of
something bigger it seems.

00:49:48.180 --> 00:49:51.210
Anyway, there are important
bilateral initiatives as well.

00:49:51.210 --> 00:49:54.300
Last year, Tokyo and Canberra signed

00:49:54.300 --> 00:49:55.770
a reciprocal access agreement

00:49:55.770 --> 00:49:58.620
to establish the two
countries defense forces

00:49:58.620 --> 00:50:01.290
to cooperate with each
other for the first time,

00:50:01.290 --> 00:50:03.510
enhanced partnerships
with European states,

00:50:03.510 --> 00:50:07.080
including the Brits who trained annually.

00:50:07.080 --> 00:50:10.890
Now with the Japanese ground
self-defense force in Japan.

00:50:10.890 --> 00:50:12.960
Last year, Tokyo and London signed

00:50:12.960 --> 00:50:14.880
a reciprocal access agreement modeled

00:50:14.880 --> 00:50:16.323
on the one with Australia.

00:50:17.190 --> 00:50:21.750
And perhaps the biggest and
for US defense contractors,

00:50:21.750 --> 00:50:26.100
the most unwelcome news was
the agreement signed late

00:50:26.100 --> 00:50:30.750
last year by the UK, Italy, and Japan.

00:50:30.750 --> 00:50:35.280
All three partners in the
Lockheed Martin F35 program

00:50:35.280 --> 00:50:38.249
to form a new partnership of equals,

00:50:38.249 --> 00:50:42.250
"To develop a sixth
generation fighter platform

00:50:43.216 --> 00:50:45.180
"for the Japanese."

00:50:45.180 --> 00:50:46.770
And for the first time,

00:50:46.770 --> 00:50:50.273
a Japanese combat plane
will not be purchased,

00:50:50.273 --> 00:50:55.273
co-developed or co-produced
with the United States.

00:50:56.940 --> 00:51:00.240
Now for its part, France
with has 2 million nationals

00:51:00.240 --> 00:51:05.240
and 8,000 military personnel
in the Indo-Pacific

00:51:06.600 --> 00:51:09.603
has strengthened its ties
with Japan and even Germany,

00:51:10.440 --> 00:51:12.540
which you wouldn't think
of as having anything

00:51:12.540 --> 00:51:15.870
to defend necessarily out in the east,

00:51:17.850 --> 00:51:19.860
in the South China Sea and so forth.

00:51:19.860 --> 00:51:22.470
It has no Pacific
territories in the south down

00:51:22.470 --> 00:51:23.970
in the Philippines Sea.

00:51:23.970 --> 00:51:28.324
Anyway, it delighted many in
Japan by sending a frigate

00:51:28.324 --> 00:51:30.420
to Northeast Asia and telling the press,

00:51:30.420 --> 00:51:33.270
this is the thing that
really got people excited,

00:51:33.270 --> 00:51:34.110
wasn't the frigate.

00:51:34.110 --> 00:51:37.203
Frigate didn't impress many
people, certainly not China.

00:51:38.160 --> 00:51:42.990
But that saying publicly
that China cannot be allowed

00:51:42.990 --> 00:51:46.203
to impose their own order in
the region, it's the Germans.

00:51:47.160 --> 00:51:47.993
And of course,

00:51:47.993 --> 00:51:51.150
none of these relationships
compares to the US Japanese,

00:51:51.150 --> 00:51:55.290
or even to the Australia
Japanese security ties.

00:51:55.290 --> 00:51:58.320
But it's worth noting how
security stopped being

00:51:58.320 --> 00:52:02.220
a third rail for aspiring Japanese leaders

00:52:02.220 --> 00:52:03.600
as China's power grew

00:52:03.600 --> 00:52:07.770
and as America's power
has declined relatively.

00:52:07.770 --> 00:52:09.150
So let me end by returning

00:52:09.150 --> 00:52:11.850
to the liberal paradigm
for a second and speak

00:52:11.850 --> 00:52:15.000
to the divisions within the
Japanese strategic community

00:52:15.000 --> 00:52:16.653
regarding national security.

00:52:18.030 --> 00:52:21.630
Although his cabinet is
responsible for the new NSS,

00:52:21.630 --> 00:52:22.920
as I've said,

00:52:22.920 --> 00:52:26.580
prime Minister Kishida
is not a military realist

00:52:26.580 --> 00:52:29.040
in the Abe mold.

00:52:29.040 --> 00:52:32.040
Indeed we recall that
he became prime minister

00:52:32.040 --> 00:52:35.730
over Abe's objections, opposition,

00:52:35.730 --> 00:52:37.590
and therefore felt it necessary

00:52:37.590 --> 00:52:39.090
as a matter of political logic.

00:52:39.090 --> 00:52:43.560
He can count faction numbers

00:52:43.560 --> 00:52:46.050
and felt it necessary as a
matter of political logic

00:52:46.050 --> 00:52:50.370
to accept a harder line NSS
than he was usually associated

00:52:50.370 --> 00:52:52.260
with after he was,

00:52:52.260 --> 00:52:55.350
he was considered dovish
in the Yoshia pragmatic

00:52:55.350 --> 00:52:56.703
in the Yoshida school.

00:52:57.540 --> 00:52:59.700
This was a struggle for him.

00:52:59.700 --> 00:53:03.870
For a time he and his supporters
took pains to remind voters

00:53:03.870 --> 00:53:06.180
that China was Japan's
largest trading partner.

00:53:06.180 --> 00:53:10.560
And right after taking
office in late 2021,

00:53:10.560 --> 00:53:13.770
Mr. Kishida declared his
government would make an effort

00:53:13.770 --> 00:53:17.400
to stabilize relations with Beijing.

00:53:17.400 --> 00:53:19.680
Immediately stepping into office,

00:53:19.680 --> 00:53:21.480
he sees the opportunity to insert

00:53:21.480 --> 00:53:23.640
a more China moderate party chief

00:53:23.640 --> 00:53:26.400
and a more China moderate
foreign minister,

00:53:26.400 --> 00:53:29.790
who actually had chaired
the China Japan Friendship,

00:53:29.790 --> 00:53:32.310
Parliamentary friendship
Committee for four years.

00:53:32.310 --> 00:53:34.410
That got some, that
got people's attention.

00:53:34.410 --> 00:53:37.500
So it was a reminder in
a way, but he still went,

00:53:37.500 --> 00:53:39.140
went along with a hard line NSS.

00:53:39.140 --> 00:53:41.550
So this is a reminder
that a range of visions

00:53:41.550 --> 00:53:44.583
among Japanese strategists
persists even within the LEP.

00:53:46.440 --> 00:53:47.910
Now, the military,

00:53:47.910 --> 00:53:50.370
the military realism that
seems to have prevailed

00:53:50.370 --> 00:53:52.620
in the production of the new NSS remains

00:53:52.620 --> 00:53:56.460
one of only four grand strategic choices.

00:53:56.460 --> 00:53:57.990
And some of you have will have heard me.

00:53:57.990 --> 00:54:01.560
I hope or I have read that
I've written about this

00:54:01.560 --> 00:54:02.393
in the past.

00:54:02.393 --> 00:54:04.080
I'll, this is very briefly flick.

00:54:04.080 --> 00:54:07.110
But these realists are the folks who,

00:54:07.110 --> 00:54:08.580
that the realists are the folks who know

00:54:08.580 --> 00:54:10.020
they can't balance China alone.

00:54:10.020 --> 00:54:12.153
And while claiming to be all
in with the United States

00:54:12.153 --> 00:54:14.073
have been busy developing,

00:54:15.900 --> 00:54:20.700
busy diversifying Japan's
dependence on Washington,

00:54:20.700 --> 00:54:22.650
but they're also band wagoners,

00:54:22.650 --> 00:54:25.560
bandwagons with China in Japan,

00:54:25.560 --> 00:54:27.600
mostly in the business
community who would prefer

00:54:27.600 --> 00:54:31.320
that Japan accommodate the Chinese power.

00:54:31.320 --> 00:54:35.550
And they see China as
the new global leader,

00:54:35.550 --> 00:54:39.150
and US' time has passed.

00:54:39.150 --> 00:54:41.370
And their numbers has
have dwindled as China

00:54:41.370 --> 00:54:45.753
has overplayed its hand,
frankly, in the region.

00:54:46.646 --> 00:54:49.170
But their view is not irrelevant.

00:54:49.170 --> 00:54:52.890
Just last fall, the vice chair of Japan's,

00:54:52.890 --> 00:54:56.010
one of Japan's leading
business associations,

00:54:56.010 --> 00:54:59.730
the Keizai Doyukai some of
you know this organization

00:54:59.730 --> 00:55:02.640
made headlines, I'm
gonna read you a quote.

00:55:02.640 --> 00:55:05.227
When he declared, "That
over the course of history,

00:55:05.227 --> 00:55:09.697
"the world's hegemon has
changed from the Netherlands"

00:55:09.697 --> 00:55:12.007
"to Great Britain to the United States,"

00:55:12.007 --> 00:55:13.987
"and now China is up for the challenge."

00:55:13.987 --> 00:55:17.227
"We are at a major turning
point in world history."

00:55:17.227 --> 00:55:20.617
"Japanese companies should
not make a hasty decision

00:55:20.617 --> 00:55:21.917
"to withdraw from China."

00:55:21.917 --> 00:55:23.970
It was a perfect reflection of this view

00:55:23.970 --> 00:55:26.100
that I'm trying to communicate to you.

00:55:26.100 --> 00:55:29.550
Now third strategic perspective
is espoused by dual hedgers.

00:55:29.550 --> 00:55:32.220
People who want to have
their cake and eat too.

00:55:32.220 --> 00:55:34.780
Like Yoshida, before he moved rightward

00:55:35.881 --> 00:55:38.190
as a matter of political protection

00:55:38.190 --> 00:55:39.490
that as I've described it,

00:55:40.560 --> 00:55:44.520
to try to keep the military
realist at arms' length.

00:55:44.520 --> 00:55:45.900
These folks think that neither

00:55:45.900 --> 00:55:47.730
all in Bandwagoning with China,

00:55:47.730 --> 00:55:51.360
nor all in dependence
upon the United States,

00:55:51.360 --> 00:55:54.570
a US which is in relative decline,

00:55:54.570 --> 00:55:56.850
are really in Japan's interests.

00:55:56.850 --> 00:55:59.130
So they would like to have it both ways.

00:55:59.130 --> 00:56:01.350
And finally, there are
those who want Japan

00:56:01.350 --> 00:56:04.320
to keep its distance epox on both houses

00:56:04.320 --> 00:56:05.430
keep its distances from,

00:56:05.430 --> 00:56:07.800
its distance from both
Washington and Beijing.

00:56:07.800 --> 00:56:12.270
They prefer autonomous defense
and would like to have Japan

00:56:12.270 --> 00:56:16.830
use the US Japan alliance as a means

00:56:16.830 --> 00:56:19.023
to transcend the US Japan Alliance.

00:56:20.850 --> 00:56:22.800
Now, like the band-wagoners

00:56:22.800 --> 00:56:25.710
the autonomous fly beneath most radars.

00:56:25.710 --> 00:56:29.671
But if you want a taste, and
I urge you to have a taste

00:56:29.671 --> 00:56:32.310
of the autonomy argument,

00:56:32.310 --> 00:56:34.860
I suggest you read the
several daily tweets

00:56:34.860 --> 00:56:39.360
that are put out by Tamogami Toshio.

00:56:39.360 --> 00:56:40.193
You'll remember he was

00:56:40.193 --> 00:56:43.890
the former air self-Defense
Force chief of staff who was,

00:56:43.890 --> 00:56:48.890
who was fired in 2008 for
insisting that Japan's aggression

00:56:49.058 --> 00:56:53.190
in northeast and southeast
Asia and the 1930s and 40s

00:56:53.190 --> 00:56:55.320
was for a just clause.

00:56:55.320 --> 00:56:58.080
Now, his position was not marginal.

00:56:58.080 --> 00:57:02.340
It was certainly not what
the Japanese government

00:57:02.340 --> 00:57:03.810
espoused was national policy.

00:57:03.810 --> 00:57:06.003
So he was, he was fired.

00:57:07.350 --> 00:57:10.650
He's gone, but he then
ran for governor of Tokyo

00:57:10.650 --> 00:57:12.183
and got 16% of the vote.

00:57:13.080 --> 00:57:14.910
That's not, it's not a majority,

00:57:14.910 --> 00:57:16.680
it's not even close to, he
couldn't form a government,

00:57:16.680 --> 00:57:18.540
but he got 16% of the vote.

00:57:18.540 --> 00:57:21.720
So this is a part of the
Japanese security discourse

00:57:21.720 --> 00:57:23.730
we can't underestimate.

00:57:23.730 --> 00:57:24.563
The point is,

00:57:24.563 --> 00:57:27.240
is not only that diversity in
Japanese strategic thinking,

00:57:27.240 --> 00:57:28.650
oh, I should say he tweets,

00:57:28.650 --> 00:57:32.310
I think I did say he tweets
all the time. Have a look.

00:57:32.310 --> 00:57:33.783
It's very interesting stuff.

00:57:35.160 --> 00:57:36.960
And the point is not only that diversity

00:57:36.960 --> 00:57:39.300
in Japanese strategic
thinking is alive and well,

00:57:39.300 --> 00:57:43.350
but also that each of these
strategic choices requires

00:57:43.350 --> 00:57:47.284
Japan to get its relationship just right

00:57:47.284 --> 00:57:50.460
with Washington and Beijing.

00:57:50.460 --> 00:57:52.560
And that's, that's what
I elsewhere called the,

00:57:52.560 --> 00:57:57.270
the goldilocks challenge to
Japan to get it just right.

00:57:57.270 --> 00:57:59.310
And I'd be happy to
explore that further in Q&amp;A

00:57:59.310 --> 00:58:01.110
but let me stop here.
I've gone on too long.

00:58:01.110 --> 00:58:03.219
Thank you very much for your attention.

00:58:03.219 --> 00:58:05.220
(audience applause)

00:58:05.220 --> 00:58:06.750
- [Student] Hi, thank
you Professor Samuels

00:58:06.750 --> 00:58:09.240
for a really interesting talk.

00:58:09.240 --> 00:58:11.640
It kind of struck me at a
certain point when you were

00:58:11.640 --> 00:58:16.640
discussing Japan's extensions
looking for other partners

00:58:17.160 --> 00:58:21.981
in information collecting
and perhaps defense service.

00:58:21.981 --> 00:58:26.310
The kind of complete lack
of discussion of South Korea

00:58:26.310 --> 00:58:29.430
and how these changes
over Taiwan have impacted

00:58:29.430 --> 00:58:32.127
the South Korean Japanese relationship.

00:58:32.127 --> 00:58:33.330
And I was wondering if you
could speak to that point.

00:58:33.330 --> 00:58:34.320
- No, it's, it's great.

00:58:34.320 --> 00:58:37.800
And, I just felt like I
had to cut off something

00:58:37.800 --> 00:58:40.260
somewhere and you've, you nailed it.

00:58:40.260 --> 00:58:42.330
I did not talk about the Korea.

00:58:42.330 --> 00:58:45.150
I talked about North
Korea only in passing.

00:58:45.150 --> 00:58:46.533
And there's been,

00:58:47.460 --> 00:58:51.330
the relationship between
South Korea and Japan

00:58:51.330 --> 00:58:55.860
hit a new rock bottom over
the last five to seven years.

00:58:55.860 --> 00:58:59.010
It's never been so, I
mean, it, has been so bad,

00:58:59.010 --> 00:59:03.360
but a new post 1965 rock bottom.

00:59:03.360 --> 00:59:06.483
65 was the normalization
of relations and like that.

00:59:08.190 --> 00:59:12.663
And part of it,

00:59:14.070 --> 00:59:17.340
part of it is due to the
fact that there are actors

00:59:17.340 --> 00:59:21.510
in both countries who
are basically the skunks

00:59:21.510 --> 00:59:22.533
in the garden party.

00:59:23.430 --> 00:59:25.053
And any,

00:59:28.020 --> 00:59:30.750
any effort to have some level

00:59:30.750 --> 00:59:35.070
of reconciliation runs into
their, these politicians,

00:59:35.070 --> 00:59:39.030
either ideological or electoral logics

00:59:39.030 --> 00:59:42.780
of using the other country,

00:59:42.780 --> 00:59:47.780
the right wing in Japan
and the left wing in Korea

00:59:48.816 --> 00:59:53.816
as foils for their own,
for their own base.

00:59:55.470 --> 01:00:00.470
And, so there's that.

01:00:00.990 --> 01:00:05.990
There's the fact that
that the well, you know,

01:00:09.150 --> 01:00:11.760
obviously the history issue, right.

01:00:11.760 --> 01:00:14.490
We call it, you're a
historian, you know about this.

01:00:14.490 --> 01:00:19.140
The history issue has
been used in that way

01:00:19.140 --> 01:00:21.270
in both countries, the Japanese,

01:00:21.270 --> 01:00:24.120
by denying much of their history

01:00:24.120 --> 01:00:28.950
and the South Koreans by
inflating in some cases some

01:00:28.950 --> 01:00:29.850
some of the history.

01:00:29.850 --> 01:00:34.521
But it's hard to inflate the
violence that was brought

01:00:34.521 --> 01:00:36.930
to Korea by the Japanese.

01:00:36.930 --> 01:00:41.930
In any event, the, there
was a, there was a lot,

01:00:42.210 --> 01:00:44.490
there was a lot wrong
with the relationship,

01:00:44.490 --> 01:00:48.588
but so long as the Korean electorate sent

01:00:48.588 --> 01:00:53.588
a left-wing government
to power and maintained

01:00:53.760 --> 01:00:56.193
a military realist government at home,

01:00:57.201 --> 01:01:00.063
it was, it was a recipe for,

01:01:00.930 --> 01:01:03.060
not disaster disasters, way too strong.

01:01:03.060 --> 01:01:06.870
But it was a recipe for
significant deterioration

01:01:06.870 --> 01:01:07.703
in the relationship.

01:01:07.703 --> 01:01:11.290
And that's now changed with
the election of President Yoon

01:01:12.300 --> 01:01:17.300
and the willingness of a
more moderate, Kishida,

01:01:17.520 --> 01:01:21.030
more moderate than say
Abe was on this issue,

01:01:21.030 --> 01:01:23.043
to try to find a solution.

01:01:24.210 --> 01:01:27.330
I think people give the
United States credit

01:01:27.330 --> 01:01:29.640
for, you know, knocking heads together.

01:01:29.640 --> 01:01:31.170
We have two treaty allies out there.

01:01:31.170 --> 01:01:32.490
They don't get along with one another.

01:01:32.490 --> 01:01:34.590
They don't share
intelligence with one another

01:01:34.590 --> 01:01:39.483
and they have the same
adversary in North Korea.

01:01:40.950 --> 01:01:44.130
I don't think that the
United States deserves

01:01:44.130 --> 01:01:45.570
the credit that it's getting.

01:01:45.570 --> 01:01:50.190
I think, the Korean electorate
and the Japanese electorate

01:01:50.190 --> 01:01:52.410
deserve the credit for this.

01:01:52.410 --> 01:01:57.060
But we'll see how far it goes
'cause there are skunks in,

01:01:57.060 --> 01:01:58.410
at that garden party.

01:01:58.410 --> 01:02:01.050
I'm not sure if that gets, but there's,

01:02:01.050 --> 01:02:03.270
there are many levels to the hostility

01:02:03.270 --> 01:02:04.350
between Korean and Japan.

01:02:04.350 --> 01:02:06.330
Some of them, again, rooted in history,

01:02:06.330 --> 01:02:08.703
but some are quite contemporary.

01:02:10.440 --> 01:02:11.580
Thanks. I mean, it's a great question.

01:02:11.580 --> 01:02:13.393
It's exactly what I didn't talk about.

01:02:13.393 --> 01:02:15.420
- [Student] I was wondering
if you could talk a little bit

01:02:16.290 --> 01:02:17.873
more about Japan's plutonium
stocks in terms of, I mean,

01:02:17.873 --> 01:02:22.140
this is really a break in the
nuclear taboo in many ways

01:02:22.140 --> 01:02:25.800
that was taken without knowledge perhaps,

01:02:25.800 --> 01:02:27.630
or the ascent of the Japanese public.

01:02:27.630 --> 01:02:31.890
I was wondering where you see
that sort of stockpile and

01:02:31.890 --> 01:02:34.650
desire to create a breakout
capability started coming

01:02:34.650 --> 01:02:36.537
into place, especially since, you know,

01:02:36.537 --> 01:02:37.950
you think things like

01:02:37.950 --> 01:02:42.090
when the PRC tested its
first nuclear device

01:02:42.090 --> 01:02:43.260
during the Tokyo Olympics.

01:02:43.260 --> 01:02:44.093
I mean, that became, you know,

01:02:45.164 --> 01:02:47.711
rather serious diplomatic crisis, right.

01:02:47.711 --> 01:02:50.760
So, you know, where where do
you see this kind of shift

01:02:50.760 --> 01:02:52.290
taking place over time?

01:02:52.290 --> 01:02:54.120
- It's a, another good question.

01:02:54.120 --> 01:02:59.120
The, we are seeing now
in the, there are two,

01:03:01.230 --> 01:03:05.580
two papers that I know of
that have written by scholars

01:03:05.580 --> 01:03:06.870
who have gone into the archives

01:03:06.870 --> 01:03:09.270
and have found that there was an awareness

01:03:09.270 --> 01:03:14.270
of what was judged then
and still is now to be

01:03:14.430 --> 01:03:17.130
the benefits of nuclear
latency all the way back

01:03:17.130 --> 01:03:18.903
into the 1950s and 60s.

01:03:20.190 --> 01:03:25.080
And so the decision to go
forward with nuclear power

01:03:25.080 --> 01:03:27.600
was a decision taken in the,

01:03:27.600 --> 01:03:31.020
with the understanding that there would be

01:03:31.020 --> 01:03:32.493
a collateral benefit,

01:03:33.360 --> 01:03:38.360
which is a viable latency,
viable nuclear hedge.

01:03:40.680 --> 01:03:43.230
So in the form of those
plutonium stockpiles.

01:03:43.230 --> 01:03:44.130
So where are they?

01:03:45.090 --> 01:03:50.090
A lot of them went off to
the French and the British

01:03:50.610 --> 01:03:53.580
and so forth for a
reprocessing and so forth.

01:03:53.580 --> 01:03:56.730
But, you know, these were
all, and some people say,

01:03:56.730 --> 01:03:57.563
well, it's, you know,

01:03:57.563 --> 01:04:01.830
it's in the possession of the
Japanese nuclear power firm,

01:04:01.830 --> 01:04:04.140
the Japanese electric utilities.

01:04:04.140 --> 01:04:06.930
But a lot of it is not.

01:04:06.930 --> 01:04:11.640
And, you know, Japan has worked very hard

01:04:11.640 --> 01:04:14.850
to establish itself as the paragon

01:04:14.850 --> 01:04:17.160
of disarmament,
non-proliferation and so forth.

01:04:17.160 --> 01:04:20.250
But it's a, it's, you know,

01:04:20.250 --> 01:04:21.540
with apologies to those of you

01:04:21.540 --> 01:04:23.820
in Japanese who study Japanese fine arts,

01:04:23.820 --> 01:04:25.980
but it's a bit of a kabuki.

01:04:25.980 --> 01:04:30.810
When 311 happened, as I
mentioned before, Morimoto saw,

01:04:30.810 --> 01:04:35.810
who was the DPJs, minister
of defense said, no, we need,

01:04:37.230 --> 01:04:40.680
we need this stuff as a nuclear threat.

01:04:40.680 --> 01:04:45.680
Ishibashi, a former minister of defense

01:04:46.620 --> 01:04:50.730
from the LDP likewise said, we need this.

01:04:50.730 --> 01:04:52.680
We must not stop nuclear power.

01:04:52.680 --> 01:04:54.150
And they campaign very hard for this.

01:04:54.150 --> 01:04:57.240
Now my favorite story on this.

01:04:57.240 --> 01:04:59.880
So the Japanese have
kept the door wide open

01:04:59.880 --> 01:05:04.096
just to be really clear and
have defended an open door

01:05:04.096 --> 01:05:06.720
for the reasons, for the reasons that,

01:05:06.720 --> 01:05:09.600
with the basic reasons they
think it's a deterrent,

01:05:09.600 --> 01:05:11.073
and it may well be, maybe.

01:05:12.330 --> 01:05:14.190
My favorite story about this was

01:05:14.190 --> 01:05:16.980
a story I read in a Japanese, in,

01:05:16.980 --> 01:05:18.830
actually it was an American magazine.

01:05:20.280 --> 01:05:22.800
I think it was a Forbes or Fortune.

01:05:22.800 --> 01:05:26.100
It was a sort of a
high-end business magazine.

01:05:26.100 --> 01:05:30.570
And it was an interview with an unnamed

01:05:30.570 --> 01:05:32.560
Japanese military official

01:05:33.510 --> 01:05:37.872
who was asked by the reporter,

01:05:37.872 --> 01:05:42.872
how long will it take Japan
to become nuclear armed?

01:05:44.310 --> 01:05:46.950
How long would breakout actually take?

01:05:46.950 --> 01:05:51.950
And the reporter wrote that
his interlocutor threw up

01:05:52.770 --> 01:05:54.420
his hands and said,

01:05:54.420 --> 01:05:59.310
we have absolutely no plans
to do anything like that,

01:05:59.310 --> 01:06:04.310
but if we did, it would be
186 days, exactly six months.

01:06:06.300 --> 01:06:08.700
And so, you know, you have this,

01:06:08.700 --> 01:06:13.500
you have this option and
that's all it is right now.

01:06:13.500 --> 01:06:17.760
And actually fielding a nuclear
weapon comes in many forms.

01:06:17.760 --> 01:06:20.220
And the German one we talked about still

01:06:20.220 --> 01:06:23.520
has the United States
with its foot on the neck.

01:06:23.520 --> 01:06:26.400
And, but there are other options as well.

01:06:26.400 --> 01:06:28.290
The British option here,

01:06:28.290 --> 01:06:31.440
renting nuclear weapons
is all sorts of things.

01:06:31.440 --> 01:06:36.440
And I think depending upon how
well the United States does,

01:06:37.050 --> 01:06:41.400
in convincing the Japanese not
to be excessively concerned

01:06:41.400 --> 01:06:44.790
about its commitment and its
capabilities going forward,

01:06:44.790 --> 01:06:46.200
you won't see much,

01:06:46.200 --> 01:06:49.680
much movement toward nuclear
breakout in the near term.

01:06:49.680 --> 01:06:50.730
But in the mid and long term,

01:06:50.730 --> 01:06:53.463
I don't think they have
much of an option, frankly.

01:06:54.570 --> 01:06:57.360
- [Student] Thank you for
very interesting talk.

01:06:57.360 --> 01:06:58.953
My question is,

01:07:00.510 --> 01:07:05.510
how do you see the new
Japanese defense posture

01:07:08.850 --> 01:07:13.850
jiving with its demographic
transition that's taking place?

01:07:15.523 --> 01:07:17.460
- Very good. Good question.

01:07:17.460 --> 01:07:21.450
I'm gonna go back to, everyone understand

01:07:21.450 --> 01:07:23.610
it's the demographic transition of Japan.

01:07:23.610 --> 01:07:24.990
It's a great question

01:07:24.990 --> 01:07:28.020
and it's one you have to
think about if you're study,

01:07:28.020 --> 01:07:29.370
if you study this stuff.

01:07:29.370 --> 01:07:31.410
I'm gonna go back to Tomogami.

01:07:31.410 --> 01:07:34.620
It's my favorite right wing interlocutor

01:07:34.620 --> 01:07:37.443
indirectly as it is.
I've never met the man.

01:07:38.820 --> 01:07:41.850
He published some data yesterday,

01:07:41.850 --> 01:07:43.833
two days ago from Densu.

01:07:44.820 --> 01:07:48.510
It's a major Japanese
public relations company.

01:07:48.510 --> 01:07:52.470
And it reported that 13, a little over 13%

01:07:52.470 --> 01:07:57.470
of Japanese were willing to
fight in a war for Japan.

01:08:01.950 --> 01:08:06.950
And then he compared that
to 80% of the Chinese

01:08:07.080 --> 01:08:11.550
who are willing to fight
for China in a war.

01:08:11.550 --> 01:08:16.550
Now, not only is that an
enormous gap in relative terms,

01:08:17.220 --> 01:08:21.603
but if you factor in
the fact that Japan is,

01:08:21.603 --> 01:08:24.330
you know, a hundred and what
is it, 120, 30 million now,

01:08:24.330 --> 01:08:29.110
and China is, God knows, 3.0, 1.34 huge.

01:08:31.170 --> 01:08:34.170
And so in absolute numbers,

01:08:34.170 --> 01:08:39.170
it's an extraordinarily
large gap, demographic gap

01:08:39.930 --> 01:08:44.040
of not just of young people,

01:08:44.040 --> 01:08:48.270
but of people willing to fight
and die for their country.

01:08:48.270 --> 01:08:50.100
And they have no conscription.

01:08:50.100 --> 01:08:55.100
So I think, and they're
leaving billets unfilled

01:08:56.850 --> 01:08:57.780
on a regular basis.

01:08:57.780 --> 01:09:00.570
They are just not filling
their recruiting targets,

01:09:00.570 --> 01:09:04.860
not by a long shot,
maybe a hundred thousand,

01:09:04.860 --> 01:09:06.330
80 to a hundred thousand.

01:09:06.330 --> 01:09:09.570
I think, I think I'm
making up those numbers,

01:09:09.570 --> 01:09:12.480
but I don't think I'm off
by an order of magnitude.

01:09:12.480 --> 01:09:17.480
There's an enormous
shortfall of, in recruitment.

01:09:20.070 --> 01:09:21.900
So I think it is a serious problem.

01:09:21.900 --> 01:09:24.660
And one way around that of course

01:09:24.660 --> 01:09:29.610
is to use military
equipment that's not manned.

01:09:29.610 --> 01:09:31.890
And so, and the Japanese understand that.

01:09:31.890 --> 01:09:35.100
So they're buying more
unmanned aerial vehicles,

01:09:35.100 --> 01:09:37.470
the more missile systems and so forth.

01:09:37.470 --> 01:09:40.350
But that's not gonna get
them where they want to go

01:09:40.350 --> 01:09:41.760
in a major conflict,

01:09:41.760 --> 01:09:43.440
especially if it's a
conflict that involves

01:09:43.440 --> 01:09:46.920
the United States and
the US wants and needs

01:09:46.920 --> 01:09:48.630
boots on the ground,

01:09:48.630 --> 01:09:51.870
which is what Richard Armitage
asked for the last time.

01:09:51.870 --> 01:09:54.750
But it's a good good
question. It's a real problem.

01:09:54.750 --> 01:09:57.360
- [Student] Yes. Thank
you Professor Samuels

01:09:57.360 --> 01:09:59.223
for sharing your insights with us.

01:10:02.640 --> 01:10:07.640
Prime Minister Kishida
visited Kyiv recently

01:10:08.250 --> 01:10:12.663
when President Xi was in Moscow.

01:10:15.030 --> 01:10:17.163
That's a major signal, obviously.

01:10:18.030 --> 01:10:22.680
Is that something that
may divert some resources

01:10:22.680 --> 01:10:27.540
from the Ukraine front to Eastern Russia,

01:10:27.540 --> 01:10:32.540
or the Russians don't care at this stage?

01:10:32.550 --> 01:10:34.654
- I'm not sure I get. What is the--

01:10:34.654 --> 01:10:37.860
- [Student] Would Russia
see this as a reason

01:10:37.860 --> 01:10:41.700
to divert resources from Ukraine war,

01:10:41.700 --> 01:10:43.739
or it's not relevant?

01:10:43.739 --> 01:10:44.572
- I don't, I don't think so.

01:10:44.572 --> 01:10:48.480
I think what the Prime Minister was doing,

01:10:48.480 --> 01:10:51.330
and you know, you know, color me cynical,

01:10:51.330 --> 01:10:53.880
but he's got the G7 coming up

01:10:53.880 --> 01:10:56.550
and he's chairing it, and it's in Japan,

01:10:56.550 --> 01:10:59.730
and he can't really have the
authority he would otherwise

01:10:59.730 --> 01:11:03.450
have as chair of the G7
meeting if he didn't get

01:11:03.450 --> 01:11:07.230
to Kyiv since every one
of the other leaders did.

01:11:07.230 --> 01:11:09.630
And so I think that's
the cynical part of me.

01:11:09.630 --> 01:11:14.630
I don't see a shift in
the deployment of forces

01:11:15.540 --> 01:11:16.650
that results from any of this.

01:11:16.650 --> 01:11:19.120
In fact, I don't think
he has the authorization

01:11:20.580 --> 01:11:25.323
and the political juice
to make that happen yet.

01:11:26.280 --> 01:11:27.270
But I think he,

01:11:27.270 --> 01:11:29.100
and he really was
desperate to get over there

01:11:29.100 --> 01:11:31.080
before the G7 got started.

01:11:31.080 --> 01:11:35.430
It was clearly all politics
on that one I think.

01:11:35.430 --> 01:11:37.500
Domestic pol well, international politics,

01:11:37.500 --> 01:11:40.320
but he wants the Japanese
people to understand he's a wor,

01:11:40.320 --> 01:11:41.920
whoops, he's a world leader too.

01:11:42.810 --> 01:11:45.330
- [Student] Thank you very
much for this talk, professor.

01:11:45.330 --> 01:11:48.380
I think you mentioned
the number of 168 days

01:11:48.380 --> 01:11:51.840
to achieve a nuclear weapon for Japan.

01:11:51.840 --> 01:11:54.570
One, it's really quick process.

01:11:54.570 --> 01:11:57.330
And I want to ask, what
will be the Chinese reaction

01:11:57.330 --> 01:12:01.410
to any signal from Japan to
achieving a nuclear weapon?

01:12:01.410 --> 01:12:04.740
If any Japanese step to this direction

01:12:04.740 --> 01:12:08.223
will push China to a war in Taiwan?

01:12:09.900 --> 01:12:14.900
- Great question. The,
first of all, I don't,

01:12:14.953 --> 01:12:19.953
I don't believe that the 186
day is a reliable number,

01:12:21.240 --> 01:12:24.471
but it was the number, it was
the answer that was quoted.

01:12:24.471 --> 01:12:27.183
I think there's the, the
Japanese have a lot to do,

01:12:28.170 --> 01:12:29.280
a lot in addition to do,

01:12:29.280 --> 01:12:31.260
but we don't know because
so much of it is shrouded.

01:12:31.260 --> 01:12:34.260
So, okay, let's say if
it's not six months,

01:12:34.260 --> 01:12:36.390
it'll be, it could be a
year or a year and a half.

01:12:36.390 --> 01:12:37.623
That's still very short.

01:12:38.850 --> 01:12:40.600
What would be the Chinese reaction?

01:12:42.570 --> 01:12:47.570
Predictably, very severe, I would think.

01:12:48.330 --> 01:12:50.250
Now the question is, what does that mean?

01:12:50.250 --> 01:12:54.030
Does it mean an attack,
a preemptive attack?

01:12:54.030 --> 01:12:58.863
I don't think so because Japan has

01:12:59.760 --> 01:13:04.380
a nuclear armed treaty
partner in the United States.

01:13:04.380 --> 01:13:06.810
So I don't imagine that
that there'd be a preemptive

01:13:06.810 --> 01:13:11.810
attack in the event that a
nuclear program is unveiled.

01:13:13.176 --> 01:13:14.610
I'm sorry.

01:13:14.610 --> 01:13:16.941
- [Student] To attack on Taiwan.

01:13:16.941 --> 01:13:18.241
- Oh, an attack on Taiwan.

01:13:21.810 --> 01:13:23.190
And you're asking about
whether the Japanese

01:13:23.190 --> 01:13:25.935
would use nuclear weapons
in the event of a?

01:13:25.935 --> 01:13:27.930
- [Student] China wants to.

01:13:27.930 --> 01:13:32.930
If China will want to attack Taiwan before

01:13:33.900 --> 01:13:35.621
the Japanese have the nuclear ability?

01:13:35.621 --> 01:13:37.290
- Oh, I see, I see, I see.

01:13:37.290 --> 01:13:38.370
No, I don't think so.

01:13:38.370 --> 01:13:40.350
I don't think that, that, that would,

01:13:40.350 --> 01:13:42.390
that would do it because I think

01:13:42.390 --> 01:13:47.130
the Chinese have a bigger adversary

01:13:47.130 --> 01:13:50.610
to contend with and one more
likely to get into a fight,

01:13:50.610 --> 01:13:52.380
and that's the United States.

01:13:52.380 --> 01:13:54.270
So I think the calculation

01:13:54.270 --> 01:13:56.640
on whether or not, you know,

01:13:56.640 --> 01:14:00.570
they do it before the Japanese
can become a nuclear power

01:14:00.570 --> 01:14:04.050
is probably not high on the agenda.

01:14:04.050 --> 01:14:06.900
Yeah, I see the question now I'm good.

01:14:06.900 --> 01:14:09.120
- [Student] Thanks so much
for the very interesting talk.

01:14:09.120 --> 01:14:11.520
I wanted to ask you if you
could speak a little bit

01:14:11.520 --> 01:14:15.960
more about how Japan is going
to pay for defense budget.

01:14:15.960 --> 01:14:16.793
I thought it was really interesting.

01:14:16.793 --> 01:14:18.000
I hadn't heard about,

01:14:18.000 --> 01:14:20.670
now we can count the Japan
Coast Guard as part of

01:14:20.670 --> 01:14:22.860
the defense budget and that
gets us part of the way there.

01:14:22.860 --> 01:14:24.270
I'm wondering about
what your thoughts about

01:14:24.270 --> 01:14:26.130
how they get the rest of the way there,

01:14:26.130 --> 01:14:29.610
but also I'm wondering how
much you see the success

01:14:29.610 --> 01:14:31.020
of pledges like this or the NSS

01:14:31.020 --> 01:14:34.410
more generally as tied
to the fate of Kishida.

01:14:34.410 --> 01:14:36.450
So Kishida doesn't seem
like particularly popular.

01:14:36.450 --> 01:14:37.740
The policies might be popular,

01:14:37.740 --> 01:14:40.200
but he's not necessarily that popular

01:14:40.200 --> 01:14:41.760
with the public right now.

01:14:41.760 --> 01:14:43.650
Do you see these policies
as popular enough

01:14:43.650 --> 01:14:46.560
within the public and the
LDP that even if Kishida

01:14:46.560 --> 01:14:49.320
were to say, you know,
not last long enough

01:14:49.320 --> 01:14:50.430
to implement them,

01:14:50.430 --> 01:14:52.017
they'll probably go on without him, or,

01:14:52.017 --> 01:14:53.910
I'm just curious how
much you see it as tied

01:14:53.910 --> 01:14:56.700
to Kishida's own political future?

01:14:56.700 --> 01:14:59.280
- Yeah, and I think actually Kishida

01:14:59.280 --> 01:15:03.360
has been bold on this one in a way that

01:15:03.360 --> 01:15:06.420
I wouldn't have expected
in the sense that he's

01:15:06.420 --> 01:15:08.940
been in favor of tax increase.

01:15:08.940 --> 01:15:11.040
Whereas others have said, no, no, no,

01:15:11.040 --> 01:15:13.740
let's do, let's sell bonds.

01:15:13.740 --> 01:15:16.140
We'll make the children and grandchildren

01:15:16.140 --> 01:15:18.480
and foreigners pay for this, I think.

01:15:18.480 --> 01:15:22.413
And, but he went for the tax increase,

01:15:23.400 --> 01:15:25.770
but he's not got the
support that he needs.

01:15:25.770 --> 01:15:27.810
He's not got the support
he needs for that.

01:15:27.810 --> 01:15:29.010
So how do you get there?

01:15:29.010 --> 01:15:31.440
Well, it's not just the
Coast Guard, remember,

01:15:31.440 --> 01:15:36.440
it's also any government
funding for dual use

01:15:37.950 --> 01:15:40.080
or dual use technologies.

01:15:40.080 --> 01:15:43.383
And that, you know, that's a big tent.

01:15:44.220 --> 01:15:46.440
That's a huge tent
'cause almost everything

01:15:46.440 --> 01:15:50.310
is a dual use technology
according to it's proponents.

01:15:50.310 --> 01:15:52.020
You know, it's the people

01:15:52.020 --> 01:15:54.300
who are looking for the funding, right.

01:15:54.300 --> 01:15:55.860
So it's duel use.

01:15:55.860 --> 01:15:59.040
And I, you know, it would be interesting

01:15:59.040 --> 01:16:01.260
to actually do some back of the envelope,

01:16:01.260 --> 01:16:03.120
at least back of the envelope calculations

01:16:03.120 --> 01:16:05.460
on how far that gets them.

01:16:05.460 --> 01:16:07.650
But it's clearly not far enough

01:16:07.650 --> 01:16:10.470
because they're still
talking about tax and bonds.

01:16:10.470 --> 01:16:13.780
And there may be some, perhaps some

01:16:17.100 --> 01:16:18.990
agreement reached some kind of a,

01:16:18.990 --> 01:16:21.180
a negotiated settlement reach

01:16:21.180 --> 01:16:22.530
between the advocates for each.

01:16:22.530 --> 01:16:25.920
But I sort of doubt it that
the LDP has had nothing.

01:16:25.920 --> 01:16:27.420
But as you know, Charlie,

01:16:27.420 --> 01:16:29.370
the LDP has had nothing but trouble.

01:16:29.370 --> 01:16:30.750
Every time it raises taxes,

01:16:30.750 --> 01:16:34.140
it gets hammered by voters, hammered.

01:16:34.140 --> 01:16:36.030
And so it's not only they
don't wanna fight and die

01:16:36.030 --> 01:16:39.540
for their country, they
don't wanna pay taxes either.

01:16:39.540 --> 01:16:42.030
They already do pay
tax, pay a lot of taxes.

01:16:42.030 --> 01:16:43.950
They don't wanna pay more taxes.

01:16:43.950 --> 01:16:46.410
So I think he's in a bind.

01:16:46.410 --> 01:16:48.900
But I got the impression that,

01:16:48.900 --> 01:16:51.900
that his popularity has improved

01:16:51.900 --> 01:16:53.760
in the last couple three weeks.

01:16:53.760 --> 01:16:56.040
And I think getting over to Kyiv

01:16:56.040 --> 01:16:59.370
back to that question from
the gentleman up here.

01:16:59.370 --> 01:17:01.710
Getting to Kyiv and
demonstrating leadership

01:17:01.710 --> 01:17:06.060
of this sort will further
reinforce and maybe even lead to

01:17:06.060 --> 01:17:10.440
an early solution of
diet and the reelection.

01:17:10.440 --> 01:17:13.623
So that's my guess is the
calculation there. Yeah.

01:17:15.810 --> 01:17:18.360
- [Student] Am I able to? Can I ask?

01:17:18.360 --> 01:17:19.230
- So--
- Oh there you are.

01:17:19.230 --> 01:17:21.270
- [Student] Thank you very
much for a wonderful talk.

01:17:21.270 --> 01:17:23.580
And, I was trying to
think of a brave question,

01:17:23.580 --> 01:17:25.350
but I'm a comparative.

01:17:25.350 --> 01:17:26.970
I'm gonna ask about voters,

01:17:26.970 --> 01:17:28.980
and this is actually a
perfect follow up I think

01:17:28.980 --> 01:17:31.440
to Charlie's question
because I was also interested

01:17:31.440 --> 01:17:35.610
in the payment for these new programs

01:17:35.610 --> 01:17:37.893
or for the expansion
of the defense budget.

01:17:38.759 --> 01:17:40.230
And I know the IR scholars are interested

01:17:40.230 --> 01:17:41.940
in what sinks battleships,

01:17:41.940 --> 01:17:44.220
and we are interested
in what sinks the LDP.

01:17:44.220 --> 01:17:46.650
And so I'm curious, right,

01:17:46.650 --> 01:17:51.650
if the current trend among
Japanese leaders is realist,

01:17:52.680 --> 01:17:54.480
can we construe public opinion?

01:17:54.480 --> 01:17:57.510
Can we construe the
voters as well as realists

01:17:57.510 --> 01:18:00.630
or merely realists accommodating for now?

01:18:00.630 --> 01:18:02.670
And, that is to say,
when push comes to shove,

01:18:02.670 --> 01:18:05.940
is everybody a realist until
they come for your pension?

01:18:05.940 --> 01:18:07.470
- It's like the,

01:18:07.470 --> 01:18:10.383
everyone's a liberal until
they get mugged, right, right.

01:18:11.370 --> 01:18:12.210
Yeah. No.

01:18:12.210 --> 01:18:15.600
Okay. I don't know the
answer to that except to say,

01:18:15.600 --> 01:18:18.870
except to report to you something
you already know, Colin,

01:18:18.870 --> 01:18:21.960
which is that the, I keep doing that.

01:18:21.960 --> 01:18:26.830
That the Japanese voters
now report support.

01:18:27.840 --> 01:18:30.060
They're not just accommodating to Abe,

01:18:30.060 --> 01:18:31.590
they're actually supporting

01:18:31.590 --> 01:18:34.380
military realist programs and postures.

01:18:34.380 --> 01:18:36.690
That they, it seems to me real,

01:18:36.690 --> 01:18:40.860
a real shift that the
center of gravity has moved

01:18:40.860 --> 01:18:44.340
in Japanese public opinion
and that it's a real thing.

01:18:44.340 --> 01:18:48.780
And, but you know, it's also,

01:18:48.780 --> 01:18:50.250
it's also true in the United States

01:18:50.250 --> 01:18:53.550
that everybody is, you know,
let's go fight this war.

01:18:53.550 --> 01:18:56.020
We do this all the time
until it's your children

01:18:56.910 --> 01:18:58.260
or your neighbor's children,

01:18:58.260 --> 01:19:00.510
and then you're not so
enthusiastic anymore, right.

01:19:00.510 --> 01:19:03.123
So, or shouldn't be anyway.

01:19:04.350 --> 01:19:08.130
So I think that, I think it's,

01:19:09.990 --> 01:19:14.010
I think it's a real change,
I guess is the bottom line.

01:19:14.010 --> 01:19:18.670
It's substantive, it's
real, it's calculable and

01:19:21.150 --> 01:19:22.740
I think sustainable.

01:19:22.740 --> 01:19:27.330
China, China's just been, as
I said, overplayed its hand.

01:19:27.330 --> 01:19:31.860
China had, could have achieved
so much more by not being

01:19:31.860 --> 01:19:35.100
as provocative as it's been in the region.

01:19:35.100 --> 01:19:40.100
And, now during the Abe administration,

01:19:41.880 --> 01:19:44.910
Abe goes to Beijing and
he talks to Xi Jinping

01:19:44.910 --> 01:19:48.450
and they work out, a whole
list of economic agreements

01:19:48.450 --> 01:19:52.173
is October now of 2018, I think, right.

01:19:53.460 --> 01:19:55.320
And they work out a
whole set of agreements.

01:19:55.320 --> 01:19:56.790
And it looked like, you know,

01:19:56.790 --> 01:20:00.933
that Abe had moved to the
center from the right,

01:20:01.800 --> 01:20:03.630
as it turned out, nothing ever took place,

01:20:03.630 --> 01:20:05.220
nothing ever happened.
It was all for show.

01:20:05.220 --> 01:20:07.140
And so the real,

01:20:07.140 --> 01:20:11.033
the reality is everyone has
moved to the right in Japan.

01:20:12.180 --> 01:20:14.640
- [Student] Thank you so
much for your wonderful talk.

01:20:14.640 --> 01:20:17.070
So my question is on.

01:20:17.070 --> 01:20:20.190
So you mentioned how the
overall business sector of Japan

01:20:20.190 --> 01:20:22.532
right now favors increasing

01:20:22.532 --> 01:20:25.230
betrayed interactions with China.

01:20:25.230 --> 01:20:30.230
And I want to know how to
what extent it represents

01:20:30.690 --> 01:20:33.810
the sentiment, the concept,

01:20:33.810 --> 01:20:36.180
the perception of the general public,

01:20:36.180 --> 01:20:40.440
the small business owners,
especially tourism on tourism,

01:20:40.440 --> 01:20:44.100
or it represents the
wilderness of the elite class,

01:20:44.100 --> 01:20:45.120
the big companies.

01:20:45.120 --> 01:20:47.500
And to what extent can this

01:20:48.960 --> 01:20:52.980
business oriented sentiment
impact the policy making

01:20:52.980 --> 01:20:56.264
of the Japanese government?

01:20:56.264 --> 01:20:58.950
- Yeah. I mean I think
business is more divided.

01:20:58.950 --> 01:21:03.330
I mean, what I was trying to
get at was that there is a part

01:21:03.330 --> 01:21:06.640
of business like the
Keizai Doyukai spokesperson

01:21:07.530 --> 01:21:11.730
who sees China as the future and so forth.

01:21:11.730 --> 01:21:14.490
But I think business is
quite divided on China.

01:21:14.490 --> 01:21:17.460
There are those who are dependent
upon imports from China,

01:21:17.460 --> 01:21:22.140
like rare earth metals, for example,

01:21:22.140 --> 01:21:26.070
who were denied access to
them and then had to hustle

01:21:26.070 --> 01:21:28.230
to find alternative sourcing and so forth,

01:21:28.230 --> 01:21:30.030
due to political, political,

01:21:30.030 --> 01:21:32.010
bilateral political disputes, right.

01:21:32.010 --> 01:21:36.090
So you have, you have, in a sense,

01:21:36.090 --> 01:21:40.980
the Japanese business
community is split between

01:21:40.980 --> 01:21:45.980
those who built big factories in China

01:21:46.230 --> 01:21:47.940
and want them to succeed.

01:21:47.940 --> 01:21:49.890
It's an enormous capital investments.

01:21:49.890 --> 01:21:53.817
And those who are trying to extract

01:21:53.817 --> 01:21:56.193
certain raw materials.

01:21:57.030 --> 01:22:00.660
The people who are at the
top of the value added chain

01:22:00.660 --> 01:22:02.610
will not necessarily have

01:22:02.610 --> 01:22:05.850
the same set of interests
vis-a-vis trade with China

01:22:05.850 --> 01:22:08.610
as the bottom of the value added chain.

01:22:08.610 --> 01:22:11.760
I only offered the example
I offered to illustrate one

01:22:11.760 --> 01:22:14.520
particular corner of
the security discourse.

01:22:14.520 --> 01:22:17.490
I don't think it represented
all of Japanese business.

01:22:17.490 --> 01:22:21.210
It represented those who really,

01:22:21.210 --> 01:22:23.520
who really see it as China as the future,

01:22:23.520 --> 01:22:24.720
but that's not everyone.

01:22:25.890 --> 01:22:27.090
- [Student] Thank you
so much for the talk.

01:22:27.090 --> 01:22:32.090
So I have a question about
the underlying courses of the,

01:22:34.500 --> 01:22:38.747
of the change of public opinion
about the military policies.

01:22:38.747 --> 01:22:43.747
'Cause it seems at least
to me that the reason for,

01:22:43.800 --> 01:22:47.760
the reason for the change is not limited

01:22:47.760 --> 01:22:52.760
to how the Japanese like
right-wing partition emphasize

01:22:53.940 --> 01:22:55.770
the, like China threat discourse,

01:22:55.770 --> 01:22:58.560
but also it can be attributed to

01:22:58.560 --> 01:23:00.840
the loss of public resonance

01:23:00.840 --> 01:23:04.050
of one's board pacifist movement.

01:23:04.050 --> 01:23:09.050
So I would like to ask for what reason

01:23:10.470 --> 01:23:15.000
the Japanese pacific
activists came to lose

01:23:15.000 --> 01:23:20.000
the public resonance
that they wants to have?

01:23:21.148 --> 01:23:24.030
- Well, I think that,
as I said in my remarks,

01:23:24.030 --> 01:23:25.680
there were three reasons.

01:23:25.680 --> 01:23:30.680
One was that for many Japanese citizens,

01:23:32.520 --> 01:23:37.520
they recognized that China was
a security threat to Japan.

01:23:37.650 --> 01:23:40.743
That they came to see it
as Abe was painting it.

01:23:41.670 --> 01:23:42.780
And the Abe group,

01:23:42.780 --> 01:23:44.970
the military realist that I described.

01:23:44.970 --> 01:23:47.013
And so they understood that.

01:23:47.970 --> 01:23:50.790
That's kind of a highfalutin view

01:23:50.790 --> 01:23:52.770
because not everybody in, you know,

01:23:52.770 --> 01:23:55.320
in the Japanese public sees things

01:23:55.320 --> 01:23:57.720
in these kinds of strategic terms.

01:23:57.720 --> 01:23:59.940
But they're are the
three strategic reasons.

01:23:59.940 --> 01:24:02.820
One is that North Korea,

01:24:02.820 --> 01:24:05.670
I mean it's throwing
hardware over the archipelago

01:24:05.670 --> 01:24:07.350
and nuclear missiles.

01:24:07.350 --> 01:24:10.710
And he's been doing it
for 10, 12 years now.

01:24:10.710 --> 01:24:13.680
And, it's very provocative.

01:24:13.680 --> 01:24:15.840
And so they see that as a threat.

01:24:15.840 --> 01:24:17.880
So North Korea is a threat.

01:24:17.880 --> 01:24:21.210
They see China as a threat.

01:24:21.210 --> 01:24:24.063
And you know, there's a lot of,

01:24:25.020 --> 01:24:29.940
of fuss over the strategic importance

01:24:29.940 --> 01:24:32.640
of a very unstrategic bit of land,

01:24:32.640 --> 01:24:35.017
which is the Senkaku.

01:24:35.017 --> 01:24:38.560
I mean, Senkaku islands are
inside the first island chain.

01:24:38.560 --> 01:24:41.670
If the Chinese get the Senkaku islands,

01:24:41.670 --> 01:24:45.000
they still can't get out
of the first island chain.

01:24:45.000 --> 01:24:48.840
If they get Taiwan, you
know all bets are off,

01:24:48.840 --> 01:24:51.243
then they're out into the Philippines Sea.

01:24:52.080 --> 01:24:56.220
But so it's the threat
from China as they see it,

01:24:56.220 --> 01:24:57.780
the threat from North
Korea as they see it.

01:24:57.780 --> 01:25:02.780
And as I say, the demonstrable
threat of the Americans

01:25:02.970 --> 01:25:04.890
not caring much.

01:25:04.890 --> 01:25:08.220
President Trump made it very
clear, you know, within two,

01:25:08.220 --> 01:25:12.390
within a week after his inauguration,

01:25:12.390 --> 01:25:17.390
he bailed out on the TPP, the
Trans-Pacific Partnership.

01:25:18.870 --> 01:25:20.160
And what did we get?

01:25:20.160 --> 01:25:23.850
This week the British joined
the Trans-Pacific Partnership.

01:25:23.850 --> 01:25:26.910
They left BREXIT, and they
left, you know, the EU

01:25:26.910 --> 01:25:29.880
and they, joined the
trans, much bigger market.

01:25:29.880 --> 01:25:31.920
And the Americans are left out in the cold

01:25:31.920 --> 01:25:35.204
to these bilateral,
bilateral FTAs and so forth.

01:25:35.204 --> 01:25:37.200
So anyway, the point is the Americans

01:25:37.200 --> 01:25:39.220
in the Trump administration there was

01:25:40.395 --> 01:25:45.395
the dropping of the TPP,
which, you know, it you know,

01:25:45.720 --> 01:25:48.870
for domestic political reasons,
which we can talk about.

01:25:48.870 --> 01:25:50.610
Number one, the demands

01:25:50.610 --> 01:25:54.030
that host nation support
be double tripled,

01:25:54.030 --> 01:25:57.510
you know, and really telling the Japanese

01:25:57.510 --> 01:25:59.850
that they're only good for, you know,

01:25:59.850 --> 01:26:02.850
that we're only there to be
mercenaries and we'll fight

01:26:02.850 --> 01:26:05.340
for you, but you gotta
pay us a lot more money.

01:26:05.340 --> 01:26:08.940
He just didn't understand the
strategic issues, didn't care.

01:26:08.940 --> 01:26:10.683
And he was doing that.

01:26:12.870 --> 01:26:17.870
And of course, after the,
after his first visit to Korea,

01:26:19.230 --> 01:26:22.230
he was very, North Korea visit with,

01:26:22.230 --> 01:26:24.570
I should say Kim Jong-un.

01:26:24.570 --> 01:26:26.553
I guess in Singapore that summer.

01:26:28.110 --> 01:26:30.930
He made it very clear
that the United States

01:26:30.930 --> 01:26:34.200
didn't care about medium
range nuclear weapons.

01:26:34.200 --> 01:26:37.590
They cared about intercontinental
ballistic missiles.

01:26:37.590 --> 01:26:41.550
And so the result was he's,
and he said it out loud,

01:26:41.550 --> 01:26:44.070
well, of course, you
know, if you're Japanese,

01:26:44.070 --> 01:26:47.790
you know, you care about
both or you should.

01:26:47.790 --> 01:26:51.330
And you feel quite abandoned
by the United States

01:26:51.330 --> 01:26:52.860
when you get a president
of the United States

01:26:52.860 --> 01:26:54.120
saying such things.

01:26:54.120 --> 01:26:58.920
So there's, those are the
three strategic reasons,

01:26:58.920 --> 01:27:03.690
and I think they just have,
have been fed a steady diet

01:27:03.690 --> 01:27:06.450
of news about the Senkaku.

01:27:06.450 --> 01:27:10.420
I mean, you know, there's poor
goats on the Senkaku island,

01:27:10.420 --> 01:27:11.820
there's a lot of goats
there, but that's about,

01:27:11.820 --> 01:27:12.653
that's about it.

01:27:12.653 --> 01:27:17.510
Anyway, they're fed a steady
diet about imminent aggression.

01:27:18.990 --> 01:27:21.363
And you know, on some of it sticks.

01:27:22.620 --> 01:27:25.200
Some of it as we've seen in
the United States, you know,

01:27:25.200 --> 01:27:29.460
there's some of the most
outrageous outlandish things stick

01:27:29.460 --> 01:27:33.210
and you gotta deal with
it politically at home.

01:27:33.210 --> 01:27:35.070
- [Student] Thank you
for the interesting talk.

01:27:35.070 --> 01:27:36.030
So I have a question.

01:27:36.030 --> 01:27:39.960
I've wonder, I'm curious to
know the role of electoral poli,

01:27:39.960 --> 01:27:42.090
electoral competition in

01:27:42.090 --> 01:27:44.430
determining foreign policy in Japan.

01:27:44.430 --> 01:27:46.230
So maybe I missed up in the talk,

01:27:46.230 --> 01:27:50.400
but are there any factions in
LDP and do different parties

01:27:50.400 --> 01:27:53.820
have different stance on this,

01:27:53.820 --> 01:27:58.800
on China threat and non-foreign
policy in general? Yeah.

01:27:58.800 --> 01:28:01.980
- Yeah. No. Yes, the
answer is yes and yes.

01:28:01.980 --> 01:28:06.090
The LDP, since its in,

01:28:06.090 --> 01:28:08.940
since its in initiation in the 1950s,

01:28:08.940 --> 01:28:13.260
has been split between a
nationalist wing in the book,

01:28:13.260 --> 01:28:15.180
I call them the Revisionist
wing, but it's a national,

01:28:15.180 --> 01:28:16.560
they wanted to revise the constitution,

01:28:16.560 --> 01:28:17.670
but it's really should have,

01:28:17.670 --> 01:28:19.950
I should have called it
the nationalist wing.

01:28:19.950 --> 01:28:23.640
Which was Kishi Nobusuke,
Abe's grandfather

01:28:23.640 --> 01:28:25.890
and Natasone Yaskuro.

01:28:25.890 --> 01:28:27.870
And those guys.

01:28:27.870 --> 01:28:30.870
And the pragmatists under Yoshida Shigeru

01:28:30.870 --> 01:28:32.550
and his follower.

01:28:32.550 --> 01:28:36.082
And you know, there was
the pragmatists were

01:28:36.082 --> 01:28:38.910
they were pragmatic,

01:28:38.910 --> 01:28:42.180
and they didn't do things to inflame

01:28:42.180 --> 01:28:46.440
the bilateral relationships
in foreign policy

01:28:46.440 --> 01:28:49.440
in the way that the nationalists would.

01:28:49.440 --> 01:28:53.490
And in fact, it's curious because
from American perspective,

01:28:53.490 --> 01:28:55.530
it seemed that there's no,

01:28:55.530 --> 01:28:57.390
there was no better friend
of the United States

01:28:57.390 --> 01:28:59.220
than Kishi Nobusuke,

01:28:59.220 --> 01:29:03.740
which is very, very ironic
since he was a, you know,

01:29:03.740 --> 01:29:07.200
an unindicted war criminal
spent time in Sugamo prison.

01:29:07.200 --> 01:29:08.790
He was Abe's grandfather.

01:29:08.790 --> 01:29:13.050
And but you know, he was anti-communist.

01:29:13.050 --> 01:29:15.810
That was good enough for
us during the Cold War.

01:29:15.810 --> 01:29:18.390
So there are those two big groups.

01:29:18.390 --> 01:29:23.390
Now there are factions which
are not all policy defined.

01:29:26.114 --> 01:29:28.140
There's leadership.

01:29:28.140 --> 01:29:30.390
And so for example, you
get someone like Aso,

01:29:31.590 --> 01:29:35.430
former Prime Minister
and former vice prime

01:29:35.430 --> 01:29:36.870
deputy, prime minister and so forth.

01:29:36.870 --> 01:29:39.030
He's got all sorts of
people in his faction.

01:29:39.030 --> 01:29:41.316
They're not all military realists.

01:29:41.316 --> 01:29:45.390
And, those factions matter.

01:29:45.390 --> 01:29:49.860
And we saw how much they
mattered when Abbe ordered

01:29:49.860 --> 01:29:52.110
his faction or signaled to his faction

01:29:52.110 --> 01:29:53.910
that it should not support Kishida.

01:29:53.910 --> 01:29:56.970
And he had to cobble together
support from the rest of

01:29:56.970 --> 01:30:00.210
the factions to get become
president of the party

01:30:00.210 --> 01:30:01.470
and then prime minister.

01:30:01.470 --> 01:30:03.690
So these things do matter.

01:30:03.690 --> 01:30:08.021
And the Kishida folks, as I
described them in my remarks,

01:30:08.021 --> 01:30:13.021
are these dual hedgers who
would like to have it both ways.

01:30:13.200 --> 01:30:17.283
They think that they can.

01:30:18.150 --> 01:30:23.150
And so Kishida comes out
of that Yoshida school

01:30:23.520 --> 01:30:28.520
and is identified with a
much more moderate approach

01:30:32.220 --> 01:30:34.653
to China historic over the last say,

01:30:34.653 --> 01:30:35.820
well, he was foreign minister, you know.

01:30:35.820 --> 01:30:37.500
So while he was foreign minister,

01:30:37.500 --> 01:30:41.760
he was a much more deliberate,
pragmatic approach,

01:30:41.760 --> 01:30:46.050
which didn't, I don't think transform,

01:30:46.050 --> 01:30:48.450
it didn't transform Chinese behavior.

01:30:48.450 --> 01:30:50.760
And as I say, I think to the Chinese,

01:30:50.760 --> 01:30:52.020
I said it several times already,

01:30:52.020 --> 01:30:54.900
I won't repeat it, but
they miscalculate it.

01:30:54.900 --> 01:30:57.417
- [Speaker] Please join with
me in thanking Professor Samuel

01:30:57.417 --> 01:31:00.303
for his grim but enlightening talk.

01:31:01.212 --> 01:31:02.441
- Thank you.

01:31:02.441 --> 01:31:04.686
(audience applause)

01:31:04.686 --> 01:31:05.519
Thank you.

01:31:08.235 --> 01:31:10.652
(soft music)

